{{Short description|Chromatin remodeling complex}}

The '''SWI/SNF complex''', known as the '''BAF (BRG1/BRM-associated factor) complex''' in [[mammal]]s is a [[chromatin remodeling]] complex with multiple subunits that regulate gene expression by altering chromatin accessibility and DNA access for [[transcription factors]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gourisankar |first1=Sai |last2=Krokhotin |first2=Andrey |last3=Wenderski |first3=Wendy |last4=Crabtree |first4=Gerald R. |title=Context-specific functions of chromatin remodellers in development and disease |journal=Nature Reviews Genetics |date=May 2024 |volume=25 |issue=5 |pages=340–361 |doi=10.1038/s41576-023-00666-x |pmid=38001317 |pmc=11867214 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Barisic |first1=Darko |last2=Stadler |first2=Michael B. |last3=Iurlaro |first3=Mario |last4=Schübeler |first4=Dirk |title=Mammalian ISWI and SWI/SNF selectively mediate binding of distinct transcription factors |journal=Nature |date=May 2019 |volume=569 |issue=7754 |pages=136–140 |doi=10.1038/s41586-019-1115-5 |pmid=30996347 |pmc=6522387 |bibcode=2019Natur.569..136B }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kadoch |first1=Cigall |last2=Crabtree |first2=Gerald R. |title=Mammalian SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complexes and cancer: Mechanistic insights gained from human genomics |journal=Science Advances |date=5 June 2015 |volume=1 |issue=5 |article-number=e1500447 |doi=10.1126/sciadv.1500447  |doi-access=free|pmid=26601204 |pmc=4640607 |bibcode=2015SciA....1E0447K }}</ref> BAF complex remodels [[nucleosomes]] through [[ATP hydrolysis]] mediated by its [[catalysis|catalytic]] subunits BRG1 (SMARCA4) or BRM (SMARCA2).

The BAF complex exists in subtypes, canonical BAF (cBAF), BAFE (PBAF), and non-canonical BAF (ncBAF), each defined by unique subunit compositions that lead them to have specific biological functions.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gatchalian |first1=Jovylyn |last2=Malik |first2=Shivani |last3=Ho |first3=Josephine |last4=Lee |first4=Dong-Sung |last5=Kelso |first5=Timothy W. R. |last6=Shokhirev |first6=Maxim N. |last7=Dixon |first7=Jesse R. |last8=Hargreaves |first8=Diana C. |title=A non-canonical BRD9-containing BAF chromatin remodeling complex regulates naive pluripotency in mouse embryonic stem cells |journal=Nature Communications |date=3 December 2018 |volume=9 |issue=1 |article-number=5139 |doi=10.1038/s41467-018-07528-9  |doi-access=free|pmid=30510198 |pmc=6277444 |bibcode=2018NatCo...9.5139G |url=https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4kp888rm }}</ref><ref name="auto17">{{cite journal |last1=Michel |first1=Brittany C. |last2=D'Avino |first2=Andrew R. |last3=Cassel |first3=Seth H. |last4=Mashtalir |first4=Nazar |last5=McKenzie |first5=Zachary M. |last6=McBride |first6=Matthew J. |last7=Valencia |first7=Alfredo M. |last8=Zhou |first8=Qianhe |last9=Bocker |first9=Michael |last10=Soares |first10=Luis M. M. |last11=Pan |first11=Joshua |last12=Remillard |first12=David I. |last13=Lareau |first13=Caleb A. |last14=Zullow |first14=Hayley J. |last15=Fortoul |first15=Nora |last16=Gray |first16=Nathanael S. |last17=Bradner |first17=James E. |last18=Chan |first18=Ho Man |last19=Kadoch |first19=Cigall |title=A non-canonical SWI/SNF complex is a synthetic lethal target in cancers driven by BAF complex perturbation |journal=Nature Cell Biology |date=December 2018 |volume=20 |issue=12 |pages=1410–1420 |doi=10.1038/s41556-018-0221-1 |pmid=30397315 |pmc=6698386 }}</ref><ref name="auto">{{cite journal |last1=Alfert |first1=Amelie |last2=Moreno |first2=Natalia |last3=Kerl |first3=Kornelius |title=The BAF complex in development and disease |journal=Epigenetics & Chromatin |date=December 2019 |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=19 |doi=10.1186/s13072-019-0264-y |pmid=30898143 |pmc=6427853 |doi-access=free }}</ref> [[Mutations]], including the loss of function mutations, in specific subunits have been a driving factor in various human diseases, most notably [[cancer]], making BAF complexes integral in current molecular biology and research pertaining to biomedicine.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Nguyen |first1=Vinh The |last2=Tessema |first2=Mathewos |last3=Weissman |first3=Bernard Ellis |chapter=The SWI/SNF Complex: A Frequently Mutated Chromatin Remodeling Complex in Cancer |title=Epigenetics in Oncology |series=Cancer Treatment and Research |date=2023 |volume=190 |pages=211–244 |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-45654-1_7 |pmid=38113003 |isbn=978-3-031-45653-4 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Arnaud |first1=Ophelie |last2=Le Loarer |first2=François |last3=Tirode |first3=Franck |title=BAFfling pathologies: Alterations of BAF complexes in cancer |journal=Cancer Letters |date=April 2018 |volume=419 |pages=266–279 |doi=10.1016/j.canlet.2018.01.046 |pmid=29374542 |url=https://inserm.hal.science/inserm-02440494 }}</ref>

About 20% of patients with different forms of cancer had mutations in at least one BAF complex member.<ref name="auto8">{{Cite journal|title=Vulnerabilities of Mutant SWI/SNF Complexes in Cancer|first1=Katherine C.|last1=Helming|first2=Xiaofeng|last2=Wang|first3=Charles W. M.|last3=Roberts|date=September 8, 2014|journal=Cancer Cell|volume=26|issue=3|pages=309–317|doi=10.1016/j.ccr.2014.07.018|pmid=25203320|pmc=4159614}}</ref><ref name="auto7">{{cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=Xiaofeng |last2=Haswell |first2=Jeffrey R. |last3=Roberts |first3=Charles W.M. |title=Molecular Pathways: SWI/SNF (BAF) Complexes Are Frequently Mutated in Cancer—Mechanisms and Potential Therapeutic Insights |journal=Clinical Cancer Research |date=1 January 2014 |volume=20 |issue=1 |pages=21–27 |doi=10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-13-0280 |pmid=24122795 |pmc=3947303 }}</ref> In particular, these include breast cancers related to mutations in ARID1A/B, PBRM1, and ARID2,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Kexuan |last2=Wang |first2=Baocai |last3=Hu |first3=Haolin |title=Research progress of SWI/SNF complex in breast cancer |journal=Epigenetics & Chromatin |date=17 February 2024 |volume=17 |issue=1 |article-number=4 |doi=10.1186/s13072-024-00531-z |pmid=38365747 |pmc=10873968 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="auto7"/> endometriosis-associated ovarian cancers caused by mutations in ARID1A,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Fukumoto |first1=Takeshi |last2=Magno |first2=Elizabeth |last3=Zhang |first3=Rugang |title=SWI/SNF Complexes in Ovarian Cancer: Mechanistic Insights and Therapeutic Implications |journal=Molecular Cancer Research |date=1 December 2018 |volume=16 |issue=12 |pages=1819–1825 |doi=10.1158/1541-7786.MCR-18-0368 |pmid=30037854 |pmc=6279546 }}</ref><ref name="auto7"/> and renal medullary carcinomas characterized by loss of SMARCB1.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Msaouel |first1=Pavlos |last2=Malouf |first2=Gabriel G. |last3=Su |first3=Xiaoping |last4=Yao |first4=Hui |last5=Tripathi |first5=Durga N. |las