nctive mechanisms underlie the loss of SMARCB1 protein expression in renal medullary carcinoma: morphologic and molecular analysis of 20 cases |journal=Modern Pathology |date=September 2019 |volume=32 |issue=9 |pages=1329–1343 |doi=10.1038/s41379-019-0273-1 |pmid=30980040 |pmc=6731129 }}</ref> Additionally, both diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) and Burkitt lymphoma are subtypes of [[non-Hodgkin lymphoma]] originating from germinal center B cells; while DLBCL is the most common, Burkitt lymphoma is the most aggressive and clinically significant.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Metabolic Signatures Uncover Distinct Targets in Molecular Subsets of Diffuse Large B Cell Lymphoma|first1=Pilar|last1=Caro|first2=Amar U.|last2=Kishan|first3=Erik|last3=Norberg|first4=Illana A.|last4=Stanley|first5=Bjoern|last5=Chapuy|first6=Scott B.|last6=Ficarro|first7=Klaudia|last7=Polak|first8=Daniel|last8=Tondera|first9=John|last9=Gounarides|first10=Hong|last10=Yin|first11=Feng|last11=Zhou|first12=Michael R.|last12=Green|first13=Linfeng|last13=Chen|first14=Stefano|last14=Monti|first15=Jarrod A.|last15=Marto|first16=Margaret A.|last16=Shipp|first17=Nika N.|last17=Danial|date=October 16, 2012|journal=Cancer Cell|volume=22|issue=4|pages=547–560|doi=10.1016/j.ccr.2012.08.014|pmid=23079663|pmc=3479446}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Chuang |first1=Shih-Sung |last2=Ye |first2=Hongtao |last3=Du |first3=Ming-Qing |last4=Lu |first4=Chin-Li |last5=Dogan |first5=Ahmet |last6=Hsieh |first6=Pin-Pen |last7=Huang |first7=Wan-Ting |last8=Jung |first8=Yun-Chih |title=Histopathology and Immunohistochemistry in Distinguishing Burkitt Lymphoma From Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma With Very High Proliferation Index and With or Without a Starry-Sky Pattern: A Comparative Study With EBER and FISH |journal=American Journal of Clinical Pathology |date=October 2007 |volume=128 |issue=4 |pages=558–564 |doi=10.1309/EQJR3D3V0CCQGP04 |pmid=17875505 }}</ref><ref name="auto7"/> Burkitt lymphoma, in particular, is frequently linked to mutations in ARID1A and SMARCA4.<ref name="auto7"/><ref name="auto10">{{cite journal |last1=Barisic |first1=Darko |last2=Chin |first2=Christopher R. |last3=Meydan |first3=Cem |last4=Teater |first4=Matt |last5=Tsialta |first5=Ioanna |last6=Mlynarczyk |first6=Coraline |last7=Chadburn |first7=Amy |last8=Wang |first8=Xuehai |last9=Sarkozy |first9=Margot |last10=Xia |first10=Min |last11=Carson |first11=Sandra E. |last12=Raggiri |first12=Santo |last13=Debek |first13=Sonia |last14=Pelzer |first14=Benedikt |last15=Durmaz |first15=Ceyda |last16=Deng |first16=Qing |last17=Lakra |first17=Priya |last18=Rivas |first18=Martin |last19=Steidl |first19=Christian |last20=Scott |first20=David W. |last21=Weng |first21=Andrew P. |last22=Mason |first22=Christopher E. |last23=Green |first23=Michael R. |last24=Melnick |first24=Ari |title=ARID1A orchestrates SWI/SNF-mediated sequential binding of transcription factors with ARID1A loss driving pre-memory B cell fate and lymphomagenesis |journal=Cancer Cell |date=April 2024 |volume=42 |issue=4 |pages=583–604.e11 |doi=10.1016/j.ccell.2024.02.010 |pmid=38458187 |pmc=11407687 }}</ref>

== Structure and subunits ==
The BAF complex is composed of three main modules. Among these, the ATPase motor module is the driving force behind chromatin remodeling. This module uses the energy obtained from ATP hydrolysis to move nucleosomes along the DNA.<ref name="auto18">{{cite journal |last1=He |first1=Shuang |last2=Wu |first2=Zihan |last3=Tian |first3=Yuan |last4=Yu |first4=Zishuo |last5=Yu |first5=Jiali |last6=Wang |first6=Xinxin |last7=Li |first7=Jie |last8=Liu |first8=Bijun |last9=Xu |first9=Yanhui |title=Structure of nucleosome-bound human BAF complex |journal=Science |date=21 February 2020 |volume=367 |issue=6480 |pages=875–881 |doi=10.1126/science.aaz9761 |pmid=32001526 |bibcode=2020Sci...367..875H }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Grossi |first1=Elena |last2=Nguyen |first2=Christie B. |last3=Carcamo |first3=Saul |last4=Kirigin Callaú |first4=Valentina |last5=Moran |first5=Shannon |last6=Filipescu |first6=Dan |last7=Tagore |first7=Somnath |last8=Firestone |first8=Tessa M. |last9=Keogh |first9=Michael-Christopher |last10=Sun |first10=Lu |last11=Izar |first11=Benjamin |last12=Hasson |first12=Dan |last13=Bernstein |first13=Emily |title=The SWI/SNF PBAF complex facilitates REST occupancy at repressive chromatin |journal=Molecular Cell |date=2025 |volume=85 |issue=9 |pages=1714–1729.e7 |doi=10.1016/j.molcel.2025.03.026 |pmid=40252649 |pmc=12048221 |biorxiv=10.1101/2024.08.23.609212}}</ref> The core module, including ARID1A and SMARCC subunits, structures the complex and facilitates interactions with the other modules.<ref name="auto18"/><ref name="auto13">{{cite journal |last1=Mashtalir |first1=Nazar |last2=Dao |first2=Hai T. |last3=Sankar |first3=Akshay |last4=Liu |first4=Hengyuan |last5=Corin |first5=Aaron J. |last6=Bagert |first6=John D. |last7=Ge |first7=Eva J. |last8=D'Avino |first8=Andrew R. |last9=Filipovski |first9=Martin |last10=Michel |first10=Brittany C. |last11=Dann |first11=Geoffrey P. |last12=Muir |first12=Tom W. |last13=Kadoch |first13=Cigall |title=Chromatin landscape signals differentially dictate the activities of mSWI/SNF family complexes |journal=Science |date=16 July 2021 |volume=373 |issue=6552 |pages=306–315 |doi=10.1126/science.abf8705 |pmid=34437148 |pmc=8390793 |bibcode=2021Sci...373..306M }}</ref> The Actin-Related Protein module, which connects these two modules, enhances the structural integrity of the complex and ensures proper transmission of mechanical forces within it.<ref name="auto4">{{cite journal |last1=Mashtalir |first1=Nazar |last2=Suzuki |first2=Hiroshi |last3=Farrell |first3=Daniel P. |last4=Sankar |first4=Akshay |last5=Luo |first5=Jie |last6=Filipovski |first6=Martin |last7=D'Avino |first7=Andrew R. |last8=St. Pierre |first8=Roodolph |last9=Valencia |first9=Alfredo M. |last10=Onikubo |first10=Takashi |last11=Roeder |first11=Robert G. |last12=Han |first12=Yan |last13=He |first13=Yuan |last14=Ranish |first14=Jeffrey A. |last15=DiMaio |first15=Frank |last16=Walz |first16=Thomas |last17=Kadoch |first17=Cigall |title=A Structural Model of the Endogenous Human BAF Complex Informs Disease Mechanisms |journal=Cell |date=October 2020 |volume=183 |issue=3 |pages=802–817.e24 |doi=10.1016/j.cell.2020.09.051 |pmid=33053319 |pmc=7717177 }}</ref> Different combinations of the subunits that make up these modules determine the functional diversity of the complex, leading to the formation of subcomplexes such as cBAF, PBAF, and ncBAF (Table 1). Therefore, the structure of the BAF complex enables cell-specific genetic responses through specific dynamic interactions between modules and variability in subunit composition.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Yuan |first1=Junjie |last2=Chen |first2=Kangjing |last3=Zhang |first3=Wenbo |last4=Chen |first4=Zhucheng |title=Structure of human chromatin-remodelling PBAF complex bound to a nucleosome |journal=Nature |date=5 May 2022 |volume=605 |issue=7908 |pages=166–171 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04658-5 |pmid=35477757 |bibcode=2022Natur.605..166Y }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Eustermann |first1=Sebastian |last2=Patel |first2=Avinash B. |last3=Hopfner |first3=Karl-Peter |last4=He |first4=Yuan |last5=Korber |first5=Philipp |title=Energy-driven genome regulation by ATP-dependent chromatin remodellers |journal=Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology |date=April 2024 |volume=25 |issue=4 |pages=309–332 |doi=10.1038/s41580-023-00683-y |pmid=38081975 |pmc=12303036 }}</ref><ref name="auto4"/>
* cBAF: The cBAF complex, which is the most common among the others, plays a role in the initiation of gene expression. It functions primarily at enhancer regions, controlling developmental processes and playing a critical role in cell differentiation.<ref name="auto13"/><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Nocente |first1=Marina C. |last2=Mesihovic Karamitsos |first2=Anida |last3=Drouineau |first3=Emilie |last4=Soleil |first4=Manon |last5=Albawardi |first5=Waad |last6=Dulary |first6=Cécile |last7=Ribierre |first7=Florence |last8=Picaud |first8=Hélène |last9=Alibert |first9=Olivier |last10=Acker |first10=Joël |last11=Kervella |first11=Marie |last12=Aude |first12=Jean-Christophe |last13=Gilbert |first13=Nick |last14=Ochsenbein |first14=Françoise |last15=Chantalat |first15=Sophie |last16=Gérard |first16=Matthieu |title=cBAF generates subnucleosomes that expand OCT4 binding and function beyond DNA motifs at enhancers |journal=Nature Structural & Molecular Biology |date=November 2024 |volume=31 |issue=11 |pages=1756–1768 |doi=10.1038/s41594-024-01344-0 |pmid=38956169 |url=https://hal.science/hal-04639693 }}</ref>
* PBAF: PBAF contributes to the maintenance of gene transcription. It is typically localized at active [[promoter (genetics)|promoter]] regions and also functions as a [[tumor suppressor]] in certain contexts.<ref name="auto13"/>
* ncBAF: Although it also acts at enhancer regions like cBAF, unlike the other two complex types, ncBAF has a distinct structure. It is known to regulate cell type-specific gene expression.<ref name="auto13"/><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Marina-Zárate |first1=Ester |last2=Rodríguez-Ronchel |first2=Ana |last3=Gómez |first3=Manuel J. |last4=Sánchez-Cabo |first4=Fátima |last5=Ramiro |first5=Almudena R. |title=Low-affinity CTCF binding drives transcriptional regulation whereas high-affinity binding encompasses architectural functions |journal=iScience |date=March 2023 |volume=26 |issue=3 |article-number=106106 |doi=10.1016/j.isci.2023.106106 |pmid=36852270 |pmc=9958374 |bibcode=2023iSci...26j6106M }}</ref> Moreover, the unique composition of the ncBAF complex (i.e. Bromodomain-containing protein 9 -BRD9- subunit) makes it an attractive target for drug development efforts.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rej |first1=Rohan Kalyan |last2=Allu |first2=Srinivasa Rao |last3=Roy |first3=Joyeeta |last4=Acharyya |first4=Ranjan Kumar |last5=Kiran |first5=I. N. Chaithanya |last6=Addepalli |first6=Yesu |last7=Dhamodharan |first7=V. |title=Orally Bioavailable Proteolysis-Targeting Chimeras: An Innovative Approach in the Golden Era of Discovering Small-Molecule Cancer Drugs |journal=Pharmaceuticals |date=12 April 2024 |volume=17 |issue=4 |page=494 |doi=10.3390/ph17040494 |pmid=38675453 |pmc=11054475 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dreier |first1=Megan R. |last2=Walia |first2=Jasmine |last3=de la Serna |first3=Ivana L. |title=Targeting SWI/SNF Complexes in Cancer: Pharmacological Approaches and Implications |journal=Epigenomes |date=4 February 2024 |volume=8 |issue=1 |page=7 |doi=10.3390/epigenomes8010007 |pmid=38390898 |pmc=10885108 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

The three forms of the BAF complex (Figure 1) have different combinations of parts that show how each one works in a specific way. For example, the ncBAF includes SS18, GLTSCR1/1L, and BRD9; cBAF has ARID1A/B, DPF1/2/3, and SS18. SS18 is shared among these two complexes but not with PBAF. On the other hand, PBAF includes PHF10, ARID2, BRD7, and PBRM1, which are unique to it. Meanwhile, ACTL6, β-actin, BCL7A/B/C, SMARCA2/4, SMARCD1/2/3, SMARCB1, SMARCE1, and SMARCC1/2 are shared across all three complex types (Table 1).<ref name="auto17"/>
