== Biochemical mechanisms of BAF complex remodeling ==

Many remodeling enzymes utilize the energy derived from ATP hydrolysis to shift DNA along nucleosomes through its ATP-dependent DNA [[translocase]] activity. In BAF complexes, BGR1 and BRM are the catalytic subunits. After BRG1/BRM binds to the nucleosome through mechanisms such as twist diffusion<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Liu |first1=Ning |last2=Balliano |first2=Angela |last3=Hayes |first3=Jeffrey J. |title=Mechanism(s) of SWI/SNF-Induced Nucleosome Mobilization |journal=ChemBioChem |date=24 January 2011 |volume=12 |issue=2 |pages=196–204 |doi=10.1002/cbic.201000455 |pmid=21243709 |pmc=3787519 }}</ref> and loop recapture,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Zhang |first1=Yongli |last2=Smith |first2=Corey L. |last3=Saha |first3=Anjanabha |last4=Grill |first4=Stephan W. |last5=Mihardja |first5=Shirley |last6=Smith |first6=Steven B. |last7=Cairns |first7=Bradley R. |last8=Peterson |first8=Craig L. |last9=Bustamante |first9=Carlos |title=DNA Translocation and Loop Formation Mechanism of Chromatin Remodeling by SWI/SNF and RSC |journal=Molecular Cell |date=November 2006 |volume=24 |issue=4 |pages=559–568 |doi=10.1016/j.molcel.2006.10.025 |pmid=17188033 |pmc=9034902 }}</ref> it applies a mechanical force to the DNA, loosening the interactions between the histone octamers and the DNA. In this way, the opened regions of DNA become accessible to various transcription factors, [[RNA polymerase II]], and other regulatory proteins.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hao |first1=Fengxiang |last2=Zhang |first2=Ying |last3=Hou |first3=Jiayi |last4=Zhao |first4=Bin |title=Chromatin remodeling and cancer: the critical influence of the SWI/SNF complex |journal=Epigenetics & Chromatin |date=23 April 2025 |volume=18 |issue=1 |article-number=22 |doi=10.1186/s13072-025-00590-w |pmid=40269969 |pmc=12016160 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Eberharter |first1=Anton |last2=Becker |first2=Peter B. |title=ATP-dependent nucleosome remodelling: factors and functions |journal=Journal of Cell Science |date=1 August 2004 |volume=117 |issue=17 |pages=3707–3711 |doi=10.1242/jcs.01175 |pmid=15286171 }}</ref><ref name="auto1">{{cite journal |last1=Brahma |first1=Sandipan |last2=Henikoff |first2=Steven |title=The BAF chromatin remodeler synergizes with RNA polymerase II and transcription factors to evict nucleosomes |journal=Nature Genetics |date=January 2024 |volume=56 |issue=1 |pages=100–111 |doi=10.1038/s41588-023-01603-8 |pmid=38049663 |pmc=10786724 }}</ref> The subunits of this complex recognize specific [[histone]] modifications and DNA sequences: [[bromodomain]]-containing subunits (BRG1, BRM, PBRM1, BRD7/9) bind to [[acetylation|acetylated]] histones,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=Li |last2=Yu |first2=Jiali |last3=Yu |first3=Zishuo |last4=Wang |first4=Qianmin |last5=Li |first5=Wanjun |last6=Ren |first6=Yulei |last7=Chen |first7=Zhenguo |last8=He |first8=Shuang |last9=Xu |first9=Yanhui |title=Structure of nucleosome-bound human PBAF complex |journal=Nature Communications |date=10 December 2022 |volume=13 |issue=1 |article-number=7644 |doi=10.1038/s41467-022-34859-5  |doi-access=free|pmid=36496390 |pmc=9741621 |bibcode=2022NatCo..13.7644W }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Liao |first1=Lili |last2=Alicea-Velázquez |first2=Nilda L. |last3=Langbein |first3=Lauren |last4=Niu |first4=Xiaohua |last5=Cai |first5=Weijia |last6=Cho |first6=Eun-Ah |last7=Zhang |first7=Meiling |last8=Greer |first8=Celeste B. |last9=Yan |first9=Qin |last10=Cosgrove |first10=Michael S. |last11=Yang |first11=Haifeng |title=High affinity binding of H3K14ac through collaboration of bromodomains 2, 4 and 5 is critical for the molecular and tumor suppressor functions of PBRM 1 |journal=Molecular Oncology |date=April 2019 |volume=13 |issue=4 |pages=811–828 |doi=10.1002/1878-0261.12434 |doi-access=free |pmid=30585695 |pmc=6441893 }}</ref> while PHD fingers (e.g., DPF2/DPF3) specifically recognize acetylated H3 histone tails.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Matusyama |first1=Reiko |last2=Takada |first2=Ichiro |last3=Yokoyama |first3=Atsushi |last4=Fujiyma-Nakamura |first4=Sally |last5=Tsuji |first5=Naoya |last6=Kitagawa |first6=Hirochika |last7=Fujiki |first7=Ryoji |last8=Kim |first8=Misun |last9=Kouza-Fujita |first9=Madoka |last10=Yano |first10=Tetsu |last11=Kato |first11=Shigeaki |title=Retraction: Double PHD fingers protein DPF2 recognizes acetylated histones and suppresses the function of estrogen-related receptor α through histone deacetylase 1. |journal=Journal of Biological Chemistry |date=February 2015 |volume=290 |issue=8 |page=4816 |doi=10.1074/jbc.A109.077024 |pmid=25713408 |pmc=4335221 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Additionally, the ARID1A/B and ARID2 subunits bind to AT-rich DNA regions.<ref name="auto10"/><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Patil |first1=Ajinkya |last2=Strom |first2=Amy R. |last3=Paulo |first3=Joao A. |last4=Collings |first4=Clayton K. |last5=Ruff |first5=Kiersten M. |last6=Shinn |first6=Min Kyung |last7=Sankar |first7=Akshay |last8=Cervantes |first8=Kasey S. |last9=Wauer |first9=Tobias |last10=St. Laurent |first10=Jessica D. |last11=Xu |first11=Grace |last12=Becker |first12=Lindsay A. |last13=Gygi |first13=Steven P. |last14=Pappu |first14=Rohit V. |last15=Brangwynne |first15=Clifford P. |last16=Kadoch |first16=Cigall |title=A disordered region controls cBAF activity via condensation and partner recruitment |journal=Cell |date=October 2023 |volume=186 |issue=22 |pages=4936–4955.e26 |doi=10.1016/j.cell.2023.08.032 |pmid=37788668 |pmc=10792396 }}</ref> What distinguishes the BAF complex from other chromatin remodeling complexes is its ability not only to shift nucleosomes but also to completely evict histone [[octamer transcription factor|octamers]] when necessary.<ref name="auto1"/><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rawal |first1=Yashpal |last2=Chereji |first2=Răzvan V. |last3=Qiu |first3=Hongfang |last4=Ananthakrishnan |first4=Sudha |last5=Govind |first5=Chhabi K. |last6=Clark |first6=David J. |last7=Hinnebusch |first7=Alan G. |title=SWI/SNF and RSC cooperate to reposition and evict promoter nucleosomes at highly expressed genes in yeast |journal=Genes & Development |date=1 May 2018 |volume=32 |issue=9–10 |pages=695–710 |doi=10.1101/gad.312850.118 |pmid=29785963 |pmc=6004078 }}</ref> Additionally, the BAF complex functions in balance with [[polycomb-group proteins|Polycomb complexes]], which mediate [[gene repression]] through mechanisms such as H3K27 [[methylation]]. When BAF function is impaired while Polycomb activity remains intact, [[gene silencing]] persists, leading to the repression of tumor suppressor genes and potentially contributing to [[oncogene|oncogenic]] outcomes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mathur |first1=Radhika |last2=Roberts |first2=Charles W.M. |title=SWI/SNF (BAF) Complexes: Guardians of the Epigenome |journal=Annual Review of Cancer Biology |date=4 March 2018 |volume=2 |issue=1 |pages=413–427 |doi=10.1146/annurev-cancerbio-030617-050151}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Weber |first1=Christopher M. |last2=Hafner |first2=Antonina |last3=Kirkland |first3=Jacob G. |last4=Braun |first4=Simon M. G. |last5=Stanton |first5=Benjamin Z. |last6=Boettiger |first6=Alistair N. |last7=Crabtree |first7=Gerald R. |title=mSWI/SNF promotes Polycomb repression both directly and through genome-wide redistribution |journal=Nature Structural & Molecular Biology |date=June 2021 |volume=28 |issue=6 |pages=501–511 |doi=10.1038/s41594-021-00604-7 |pmid=34117481 |pmc=8504423 }}</ref><ref name="auto12">{{cite journal |last1=Panditharatna |first1=Eshini |last2=Marques |first2=Joana G. |last3=Wang |first3=Tingjian |last4=Trissal |first4=Maria C. |last5=Liu |first5=Ilon |last6=Jiang |first6=Li |last7=Beck |first7=Alexander |last8=Groves |first8=Andrew |last9=Dharia |first9=Neekesh V. |last10=Li |first10=Deyao |last11=Hoffman |first11=Samantha E. |last12=Kugener |first12=Guillaume |last13=Shaw |first13=McKenzie L. |last14=Mire |first14=Hafsa M. |last15=Hack |first15=Olivia A. |last16=Dempster |first16=Joshua M. |last17=Lareau |first17=Caleb |last18=Dai |first18=Lingling |last19=Sigua |first19=Logan H. |last20=Quezada |first20=Michael A. |last21=Stanton |first21=Ann-Catherine J. |last22=Wyatt |first22=Meghan |last23=Kalani |first23=Zohra |last24=Goodale |first24=Amy |last25=Vazquez |first25=Francisca |last26=Piccioni |first26=Federica |last27=Doench |first27=John G. |last28=Root |first28=David E. |last29=Anastas |first29=Jamie N. |last30=Jones |first30=Kristen L. |last31=Conway |first31=Amy Saur |last32=Stopka |first32=Sylwia |last33=Regan |first33=Michael S. |last34=Liang |first34=Yu |last35=Seo |first35=Hyuk-Soo |last36=Song |first36=Kijun |last37=Bashyal |first37=Puspalata |last38=Jerome |first38=William P. |last39=Mathewson |first39=Nathan D. |last40=Dhe-Paganon |first40=Sirano |last41=Suvà |first41=Mario L. |last42=Carcaboso |first42=Angel M. |last43=Lavarino |first43=Cinzia |last44=Mora |first44=Jaume |last45=Nguyen |first45=Quang-De |last46=Ligon |first46=Keith L. |last47=Shi |first47=Yang |last48=Agnihotri |first48=Sameer |last49=Agar |first49=Nathalie Y.R. |last50=Stegmaier |first50=Kimberly |last51=Stiles |first51=Charles D. |last52=Monje |first52=Michelle |last53=Golub |first53=Todd R. |last54=Qi |first54=Jun |last55=Filbin |first55=Mariella G. |title=BAF Complex Maintains Glioma Stem Cells in Pediatric H3K27M Glioma |journal=Cancer Discovery |date=11 November 2022 |volume=12 |issue=12 |pages=OF1–OF26 |doi=10.1158/2159-8290.CD-21-1491 |pmid=36305736 |pmc=9716260 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kadoch |first1=Cigall |last2=Williams |first2=Robert T |last3=Calarco |first3=Joseph P |last4=Miller |first4=Erik L |last5=Weber |first5=Christopher M |last6=Braun |first6=Simon M G |last7=Pulice |first7=John L |last8=Chory |first8=Emma J |last9=Crabtree |first9=Gerald R |title=Dynamics of BAF–Polycomb complex opposition on heterochromatin in normal and oncogenic states |journal=Nature Genetics |date=February 2017 |volume=49 |issue=2 |pages=213–222 |doi=10.1038/ng.3734 |pmid=27941796 |pmc=5285326 }}</ref>

The BAF complex in cells plays roles in various processes such as [[cell differentiation]], development, and maintenance of viability. Specifically, subunits like BRG1 and SMARCC1 maintain pluripotency,<ref name="auto6">{{cite journal |last1=Ho |first1=Lena |last2=Jothi |first2=Raja |last3=Ronan |first3=Jehnna L. |last4=Cui |first4=Kairong |last5=Zhao |first5=Keji |last6=Crabtree |first6=Gerald R. |title=An embryonic stem cell chromatin remodeling complex, esBAF, is an essential component of the core pluripotency transcriptional network |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=31 March 2009 |volume=106 |issue=13 |pages=5187–5191 |doi=10.1073/pnas.0812888106 |pmid=19279218 |pmc=2654397 |bibcode=2009PNAS..106.5187H |doi-access=free }}</ref> subunit switching in neuronal cells enables neuronal differentiation,<ref name="auto2">{{cite journal |last1=Lessard |first1=Julie |last2=Wu |first2=Jiang I. |last3=Ranish |first3=Jeffrey A. |last4=Wan |first4=Mimi |last5=Winslow |first5=Monte M. |last6=Staahl |first6=Brett T. |last7=Wu |first7=Hai |last8=Aebersold |first8=Ruedi |last9=Graef |first9=Isabella A. |last10=Crabtree |first10=Gerald R. |title=An Essential Switch in Subunit Composition of a Chromatin Remodeling Complex during Neural Development |journal=Neuron |date=July 2007 |volume=55 |issue=2 |pages=201–215 |doi=10.1016/j.neuron.2007.06.019 |pmid=17640523 |pmc=2674110 }}</ref> and it regulates tissue-specific gene expression in muscle and heart.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Ma |first1=Zhaoru |last2=Tan |first2=Shuping |last3=Lu |first3=Renhong |last4=Chen |first4=Peixin |last5=Hu |first5=Yukun |last6=Yang |first6=Tenghui |last7=Wu |first7=Hao |last8=Zhu |first8=Zhexin |last9=Guo |first9=Jiayi |last10=Chen |first10=Xi |last11=Yang |first11=Jian |last12=Zhang |first12=Wensheng |last13=Ye |first13=Ying |title=Interplay of chromatin remodeling BAF complexes in mouse embryonic and epiblast stem cell conversion and maintenance |journal=Journal of Biological Chemistry |date=February 2025 |volume=301 |issue=2 |article-number=108140 |doi=10.1016/j.jbc.2024.108140 |pmid=39730061 |pmc=11791114 |bibcode=2025JBiCh.301j8140M |doi-access=free }}</ref> In adult cells, the complex functions in DNA damage repair, [[cell cycle]] control, and preservation of cell identity.<ref name="auto12"/><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dunaief |first1=Joshua L. |last2=Strober |first2=Bruce E. |last3=Guha |first3=Sushovan |last4=Khavari |first4=Paul A. |last5=Ålin |first5=Kimona |last6=Luban |first6=Jeremy |last7=Begemann |first7=Martin |last8=Crabtree |first8=Gerald R. |last9=Goff |first9=Stephen P. |title=The retinoblastoma protein and BRG1 form a complex and cooperate to induce cell cycle arrest |journal=Cell |date=October 1994 |volume=79 |issue=1 |pages=119–130 |doi=10.1016/0092-8674(94)90405-7 |pmid=7923370 }}</ref><ref name="auto3">{{cite journal |last1=Kadoch |first1=Cigall |last2=Hargreaves |first2=Diana C |last3=Hodges |first3=Courtney |last4=Elias |first4=Laura |last5=Ho |first5=Lena |last6=Ranish |first6=Jeff |last7=Crabtree |first7=Gerald R |title=Proteomic and bioinformatic analysis of mammalian SWI/SNF complexes identifies extensive roles in human malignancy |journal=Nature Genetics |date=June 2013 |volume=45 |issue=6 |pages=592–601 |doi=10.1038/ng.2628 |pmid=23644491 |pmc=3667980 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Ho |first1=Lena |last2=Crabtree |first2=Gerald R. |title=Chromatin remodelling during development |journal=Nature |date=January 2010 |volume=463 |issue=7280 |pages=474–484 |doi=10.1038/nature08911 |pmid=20110991 |pmc=3060774 |bibcode=2010Natur.463..474H }}</ref>

== Roles in disease ==

The '''mammalian BAF complex''' is disrupted frequently in human disease, and mutations in the subunits of the BAF complex have been responsible for causing roughly 20% of all human tumors.<ref name="auto3"/><ref name="auto8"/> Subunits of the BAF complex like ARID1A, SMARCB1, SMARCA4, PBRM1, and ARID2 are considered to be tumor suppressors and are often affected by loss of function mutations across cancers.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Schick |first1=Sandra |last2=Rendeiro |first2=André F. |last3=Runggatscher |first3=Kathrin |last4=Ringler |first4=Anna |last5=Boidol |first5=Bernd |last6=Hinkel |first6=Melanie |last7=Májek |first7=Peter |last8=Vulliard |first8=Loan |last9=Penz |first9=Thomas |last10=Parapatics |first10=Katja |last11=Schmidl |first11=Christian |last12=Menche |first12=Jörg |last13=Boehmelt |first13=Guido |last14=Petronczki |first14=Mark |last15=Müller |first15=André C. |last16=Bock |first16=Christoph |last17=Kubicek |first17=Stefan |title=Systematic characterization of BAF mutations provides insights into intracomplex synthetic lethalities in human cancers |journal=Nature Genetics |date=September 2019 |volume=51 |issue=9 |pages=1399–1410 |doi=10.1038/s41588-019-0477-9 |pmid=31427792 |pmc=6952272 }}</ref><ref name="auto11">{{cite journal |last1=Shain |first1=A. Hunter |last2=Pollack |first2=Jonathan R. |title=The Spectrum of SWI/SNF Mutations, Ubiquitous in Human Cancers |journal=PLOS ONE |date=23 January 2013 |volume=8 |issue=1 |article-number=e55119 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0055119 |pmid=23355908 |pmc=3552954 |bibcode=2013PLoSO...855119S |doi-access=free }}</ref>

Because the BAF complex is modular, phenotypes in disease can indicate which subunit is altered.<ref name="auto"/> Different subunits direct targeting and activity at [[enhancer (genetics)|enhancers]], so mutations in different subunits have different methods, impact on [[tumorigenesis]], and therapeutic vulnerabilities.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hu |first1=Zhenhua |last2=Tee |first2=Wee-Wei |title=Enhancers and chromatin structures: regulatory hubs in gene expression and diseases |journal=Bioscience Reports |date=30 April 2017 |volume=37 |issue=2 |article-number=BSR20160183 |doi=10.1042/BSR20160183 |pmid=28351896 |pmc=5408663 }}</ref> Through destabilizing the cBAF complex and the accessibility of enhancers, ARID1A, for instance, is mutated in nearly half of all ovarian clear-cell and endometrioid carcinomas and is associated with loss-of-function defects that occur early in the progression of malignant transformation of endometriosis.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wiegand |first1=Kimberly C. |last2=Shah |first2=Sohrab P. |last3=Al-Agha |first3=Osama M. |last4=Zhao |first4=Yongjun |last5=Tse |first5=Kane |last6=Zeng |first6=Thomas |last7=Senz |first7=Janine |last8=McConechy |first8=Melissa K. |last9=Anglesio |first9=Michael S. |last10=Kalloger |first10=Steve E. |last11=Yang |first11=Winnie |last12=Heravi-Moussavi |first12=Alireza |last13=Giuliany |first13=Ryan |last14=Chow |first14=Christine |last15=Fee |first15=John |last16=Zayed |first16=Abdalnasser |last17=Prentice |first17=Leah |last18=Melnyk |first18=Nataliya |last19=Turashvili |first19=Gulisa |last20=Delaney |first20=Allen D. |last21=Madore |first21=Jason |last22=Yip |first22=Stephen |last23=McPherson |first23=Andrew W. |last24=Ha |first24=Gavin |last25=Bell |first25=Lynda |last26=Fereday |first26=Sian |last27=Tam |first27=Angela |last28=Galletta |first28=Laura |last29=Tonin |first29=Patricia N. |last30=Provencher |first30=Diane |last31=Miller |first31=Dianne |last32=Jones |first32=Steven J.M. |last33=Moore |first33=Richard A. |last34=Morin |first34=Gregg B. |last35=Oloumi |first35=Arusha |last36=Boyd |first36=Niki |last37=Aparicio |first37=Samuel A. |last38=Shih |first38=Ie-Ming |last39=Mes-Masson |first39=Anne-Marie |last40=Bowtell |first40=David D. |last41=Hirst |first41=Martin |last42=Gilks |first42=Blake |last43=Marra |first43=Marco A. |last44=Huntsman |first44=David G. |title=ARID1A Mutations in Endometriosis-Associated Ovarian Carcinomas |journal=New England Journal of Medicine |date=14 October 2010 |volume=363 |issue=16 |pages=1532–1543 |doi=10.1056/NEJMoa1008433 |pmid=20942669 |pmc=2976679 }}</ref> The majority of ARID1A mutations are truncating, leading to the protein being lost; however, without ARID1A, cBAF shifts to using ARID1B, which has far less abundance at enhancers, all the while decreasing the accessibility of the chromatin.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kelso |first1=Timothy W R |last2=Porter |first2=Devin K |last3=Amaral |first3=Maria Luisa |last4=Shokhirev |first4=Maxim N |last5=Benner |first5=Christopher |last6=Hargreaves |first6=Diana C |title=Chromatin accessibility underlies synthetic lethality of SWI/SNF subunits in ARID1A-mutant cancers |journal=eLife |date=2 October 2017 |volume=6 |article-number=e30506 |doi=10.7554/eLife.30506 |pmid=28967863 |pmc=5643100 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="auto5">{{cite journal |last1=Mandal |first1=Jayaprakash |last2=Mandal |first2=Prativa |last3=Wang |first3=Tian-Li |last4=Shih |first4=Ie-Ming |title=Treating ARID1A mutated cancers by harnessing synthetic lethality and DNA damage response |journal=Journal of Biomedical Science |date=19 September 2022 |volume=29 |issue=1 |article-number=71 |doi=10.1186/s12929-022-00856-5 |pmid=36123603 |pmc=9484255 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

Beyond defects that target enhancers, the loss of structural subunits removes the platform that recruits BRG1/BRM onto the chromatin, diminishing remodeling at genes involved in [[cell cycle checkpoint|checkpoint]] and differentiation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rago |first1=Florencia |last2=Rodrigues |first2=Lindsey Ulkus |last3=Bonney |first3=Megan |last4=Sprouffske |first4=Kathleen |last5=Kurth |first5=Esther |last6=Elliott |first6=GiNell |last7=Ambrose |first7=Jessi |last8=Aspesi |first8=Peter |last9=Oborski |first9=Justin |last10=Chen |first10=Julie T. |last11=McDonald |first11=E. Robert |last12=Mapa |first12=Felipa A. |last13=Ruddy |first13=David A. |last14=Kauffmann |first14=Audrey |last15=Abrams |first15=Tinya |last16=Bhang |first16=Hyo-eun C. |last17=Jagani |first17=Zainab |title=Exquisite Sensitivity to Dual BRG1/BRM ATPase Inhibitors Reveals Broad SWI/SNF Dependencies in Acute Myeloid Leukemia |journal=Molecular Cancer Research |date=1 March 2022 |volume=20 |issue=3 |pages=361–372 |doi=10.1158/1541-7786.MCR-21-0390 |pmid=34799403 }}</ref> Particularly, SMARCB1 is biallelically inactivated in nearly all malignant rhabdoid tumors, which demonstrates one of the first genetic links between loss of the BAF complex and severe pediatric cancers.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lee |first1=Ryan S. |last2=Roberts |first2=Charles W. M. |title=Rhabdoid Tumors: An Initial Clue to the Role of Chromatin Remodeling in Cancer |journal=Brain Pathology |date=March 2013 |volume=23 |issue=2 |pages=200–205 |doi=10.1111/bpa.12021 |doi-access=free |pmid=23432645 |pmc=8029057 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Versteege |first1=Isabella |last2=Sévenet |first2=Nicolas |last3=Lange |first3=Julian |last4=Rousseau-Merck |first4=Marie-Françoise |last5=Ambros |first5=Peter |last6=Handgretinger |first6=Rupert |last7=Aurias |first7=Alain |last8=Delattre |first8=Olivier |title=Truncating mutations of hSNF5/INI1 in aggressive paediatric cancer |journal=Nature |date=July 1998 |volume=394 |issue=6689 |pages=203–206 |doi=10.1038/28212 |pmid=9671307 |bibcode=1998Natur.394..203V }}</ref> SMARCA4 loss of function can disable the very ATPase involved in remodeling in BAF and cause [[small cell carcinoma]] of the ovary, hypercalcemic type, while recurring as mutations in non-small cell lung cancers, where the restriction of nucleosome mobility can silence the existing tumor suppressor genes.<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/nrclinonc.2014.63|title=SMARCA4 mutated in SCCOHT|first=Alessia|last=Errico|date=June 13, 2014|journal=Nature Reviews Clinical Oncology|volume=11|issue=6|page=302|via=www.nature.com|doi=10.1038/nrclinonc.2014.63 |pmid=24732945 |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

Carcinogenic potential is not restricted to the cBAF complex, however, since over 40% of clear-cell renal carcinomas are characterized by PBRM1 deletions/truncations.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Carril-Ajuria |first1=Lucía |last2=Santos |first2=María |last3=Roldán-Romero |first3=Juan María |last4=Rodriguez-Antona |first4=Cristina |last5=de Velasco |first5=Guillermo |title=Prognostic and Predictive Value of PBRM1 in Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma |journal=Cancers |date=19 December 2019 |volume=12 |issue=1 |page=16 |doi=10.3390/cancers12010016 |pmid=31861590 |pmc=7016957 |doi-access=free }}</ref> The loss of bromodomain in PBRM1 reduces the ability to recognize acetylated histones, causing a change in gene expression.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Varela |first1=Ignacio |last2=Tarpey |first2=Patrick |last3=Raine |first3=Keiran |last4=Huang |first4=Dachuan |last5=Ong |first5=Choon Kiat |last6=Stephens |first6=Philip |last7=Davies |first7=Helen |last8=Jones |first8=David |last9=Lin |first9=Meng-Lay |last10=Teague |first10=Jon |last11=Bignell |first11=Graham |last12=Butler |first12=Adam |last13=Cho |first13=Juok |last14=Dalgliesh |first14=Gillian L. |last15=Galappaththige |first15=Danushka |last16=Greenman |first16=Chris |last17=Hardy |first17=Claire |last18=Jia |first18=Mingming |last19=Latimer |first19=Calli |last20=Lau |first20=King Wai |last21=Marshall |first21=John |last22=McLaren |first22=Stuart |last23=Menzies |first23=Andrew |last24=Mudie |first24=Laura |last25=Stebbings |first25=Lucy |last26=Largaespada |first26=David A. |last27=Wessels |first27=L. F. A. |last28=Richard |first28=Stephane |last29=Kahnoski |first29=Richard J. |last30=Anema |first30=John |last31=A.Tuveson |first31=David |last32=Perez-Mancera |first32=Pedro A. |last33=Mustonen |first33=Ville |last34=Fischer |first34=Andrej |last35=Adams |first35=David J. |last36=Rust |first36=Alistair |last37=Chan-on |first37=Waraporn |last38=Subimerb |first38=Chutima |last39=Dykema |first39=Karl |last40=Furge |first40=Kyle |last41=Campbell |first41=Peter J. |last42=Teh |first42=Bin Tean |last43=Stratton |first43=Michael R. |last44=Futreal |first44=P. Andrew |title=Exome sequencing identifies frequent mutation of the SWI/SNF complex gene PBRM1 in renal carcinoma |journal=Nature |date=27 January 2011 |volume=469 |issue=7331 |pages=539–542 |doi=10.1038/nature09639 |pmid=21248752 |pmc=3030920 |bibcode=2011Natur.469..539V }}</ref> Although they are less frequent, ARID2 mutations, also disrupting PBAF-derived chromatin regulation, play a part in [[hepatocellular carcinoma]] development like that of hepatitis C through defectively recruiting chromatin remodelers to the tumor suppressors that restrain uncontrolled proliferation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Meng |last2=Zhao |first2=Hong |last3=Zhang |first3=Xiaosong |last4=Wood |first4=Laura D |last5=Anders |first5=Robert A |last6=Choti |first6=Michael A |last7=Pawlik |first7=Timothy M |last8=Daniel |first8=Hubert D |last9=Kannangai |first9=Rajesh |last10=Offerhaus |first10=G Johan A |last11=Velculescu |first11=Victor E |last12=Wang |first12=Linfang |last13=Zhou |first13=Shibin |last14=Vogelstein |first14=Bert |last15=Hruban |first15=Ralph H |last16=Papadopoulos |first16=Nick |last17=Cai |first17=Jianqiang |last18=Torbenson |first18=Michael S |last19=Kinzler |first19=Kenneth W |title=Inactivating mutations of the chromatin remodeling gene ARID2 in hepatocellular carcinoma |journal=Nature Genetics |date=September 2011 |volume=43 |issue=9 |pages=828–829 |doi=10.1038/ng.903 |pmid=21822264 |pmc=3163746 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Loesch |first1=Robin |last2=Chenane |first2=Linda |last3=Colnot |first3=Sabine |title=ARID2 Chromatin Remodeler in Hepatocellular Carcinoma |journal=Cells |date=23 September 2020 |volume=9 |issue=10 |page=2152 |doi=10.3390/cells9102152 |pmid=32977645 |pmc=7598172 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

