e specific roles of this remodeling machinery in human neural development.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tsurusaki |first1=Yoshinori |last2=Okamoto |first2=Nobuhiko |last3=Ohashi |first3=Hirofumi |last4=Kosho |first4=Tomoki |last5=Imai |first5=Yoko |last6=Hibi-Ko |first6=Yumiko |last7=Kaname |first7=Tadashi |last8=Naritomi |first8=Kenji |last9=Kawame |first9=Hiroshi |last10=Wakui |first10=Keiko |last11=Fukushima |first11=Yoshimitsu |last12=Homma |first12=Tomomi |last13=Kato |first13=Mitsuhiro |last14=Hiraki |first14=Yoko |last15=Yamagata |first15=Takanori |last16=Yano |first16=Shoji |last17=Mizuno |first17=Seiji |last18=Sakazume |first18=Satoru |last19=Ishii |first19=Takuma |last20=Nagai |first20=Toshiro |last21=Shiina |first21=Masaaki |last22=Ogata |first22=Kazuhiro |last23=Ohta |first23=Tohru |last24=Niikawa |first24=Norio |last25=Miyatake |first25=Satoko |last26=Okada |first26=Ippei |last27=Mizuguchi |first27=Takeshi |last28=Doi |first28=Hiroshi |last29=Saitsu |first29=Hirotomo |last30=Miyake |first30=Noriko |last31=Matsumoto |first31=Naomichi |title=Mutations affecting components of the SWI/SNF complex cause Coffin-Siris syndrome |journal=Nature Genetics |date=April 2012 |volume=44 |issue=4 |pages=376–378 |doi=10.1038/ng.2219 |pmid=22426308 }}</ref> By contrast, [[Nicolaides–Baraitser syndrome]] is caused primarily by de novo missense variants in SMARCA2, which produce severe intellectual disability, distal-limb anomalies, and sparse hair.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Van Houdt |first1=Jeroen K J |last2=Nowakowska |first2=Beata Anna |last3=Sousa |first3=Sérgio B |last4=van Schaik |first4=Barbera D C |last5=Seuntjens |first5=Eve |last6=Avonce |first6=Nelson |last7=Sifrim |first7=Alejandro |last8=Abdul-Rahman |first8=Omar A |last9=van den Boogaard |first9=Marie-José H |last10=Bottani |first10=Armand |last11=Castori |first11=Marco |last12=Cormier-Daire |first12=Valérie |last13=Deardorff |first13=Matthew A |last14=Filges |first14=Isabel |last15=Fryer |first15=Alan |last16=Fryns |first16=Jean-Pierre |last17=Gana |first17=Simone |last18=Garavelli |first18=Livia |last19=Gillessen-Kaesbach |first19=Gabriele |last20=Hall |first20=Bryan D |last21=Horn |first21=Denise |last22=Huylebroeck |first22=Danny |last23=Klapecki |first23=Jakub |last24=Krajewska-Walasek |first24=Malgorzata |last25=Kuechler |first25=Alma |last26=Lines |first26=Matthew A |last27=Maas |first27=Saskia |last28=MacDermot |first28=Kay D |last29=McKee |first29=Shane |last30=Magee |first30=Alex |last31=de Man |first31=Stella A |last32=Moreau |first32=Yves |last33=Morice-Picard |first33=Fanny |last34=Obersztyn |first34=Ewa |last35=Pilch |first35=Jacek |last36=Rosser |first36=Elizabeth |last37=Shannon |first37=Nora |last38=Stolte-Dijkstra |first38=Irene |last39=Van Dijck |first39=Patrick |last40=Vilain |first40=Catheline |last41=Vogels |first41=Annick |last42=Wakeling |first42=Emma |last43=Wieczorek |first43=Dagmar |last44=Wilson |first44=Louise |last45=Zuffardi |first45=Orsetta |last46=van Kampen |first46=Antoine H C |last47=Devriendt |first47=Koenraad |last48=Hennekam |first48=Raoul |last49=Vermeesch |first49=Joris Robert |title=Heterozygous missense mutations in SMARCA2 cause Nicolaides-Baraitser syndrome |journal=Nature Genetics |date=April 2012 |volume=44 |issue=4 |pages=445–449 |doi=10.1038/ng.1105 |pmid=22366787 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sousa |first1=Sérgio B. |last2=Hennekam |first2=Raoul C. |title=Phenotype and genotype in Nicolaides–Baraitser syndrome |journal=American Journal of Medical Genetics Part C: Seminars in Medical Genetics |date=September 2014 |volume=166 |issue=3 |pages=302–314 |doi=10.1002/ajmg.c.31409 |pmid=25169058 }}</ref>

=== Role of ARID1A as tumor suppressor ===
ARID1A, as part of cBAF, possesses an essential tumor-suppressing activity that is frequently mutated in various human cancers, including in 46–57% of ovarian [[clear cell carcinomas]] and 30% of endometrioid carcinomas as well as in prostate, breast, pancreatic and more cancers.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Takeda |first1=Takashi |last2=Banno |first2=Kouji |last3=Okawa |first3=Ryuichiro |last4=Yanokura |first4=Megumi |last5=Iijima |first5=Moito |last6=Irie-Kunitomi |first6=Haruko |last7=Nakamura |first7=Kanako |last8=Iida |first8=Miho |last9=Adachi |first9=Masataka |last10=Umene |first10=Kiyoko |last11=Nogami |first11=Yuya |last12=Masuda |first12=Kenta |last13=Kobayashi |first13=Yusuke |last14=Tominaga |first14=Eiichiro |last15=Aoki |first15=Daisuke |title=ARID1A gene mutation in ovarian and endometrial cancers (Review) |journal=Oncology Reports |date=February 2016 |volume=35 |issue=2 |pages=607–613 |doi=10.3892/or.2015.4421 |pmid=26572704 |pmc=4689482 }}</ref> One of ARID1A's several pivotal functions is its ability to direct cBAF to the enhancers, promoting oncogenesis through faulty genomic regulation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mathur |first1=Radhika |last2=Alver |first2=Burak H |last3=San Roman |first3=Adrianna K |last4=Wilson |first4=Boris G |last5=Wang |first5=Xiaofeng |last6=Agoston |first6=Agoston T |last7=Park |first7=Peter J |last8=Shivdasani |first8=Ramesh A |last9=Roberts |first9=Charles W M |title=ARID1A loss impairs enhancer-mediated gene regulation and drives colon cancer in mice |journal=Nature Genetics |date=February 2017 |volume=49 |issue=2 |pages=296–302 |doi=10.1038/ng.3744 |pmid=27941798 |pmc=5285448 }}</ref> Although ARID1A loss on its own has been shown as insufficient for tumorigenesis, it can induce ovarian [[adenocarcinomas]] when paired with PIK3CA activation, after having released IL-6 and activated [[NF-κB]].<ref name="auto14">{{cite journal |last1=Chandler |first1=Ronald L. |last2=Damrauer |first2=Jeffrey S. |last3=Raab |first3=Jesse R. |last4=Schisler |first4=Jonathan C. |last5=Wilkerson |first5=Matthew D. |last6=Didion |first6=John P. |last7=Starmer |first7=Joshua |last8=Serber |first8=Daniel |last9=Yee |first9=Della |last10=Xiong |first10=Jessie |last11=Darr |first11=David B. |last12=de Villena |first12=Fernando Pardo-Manuel |last13=Kim |first13=William Y. |last14=Magnuson |first14=Terry |title=Coexistent ARID1A–PIK3CA mutations promote ovarian clear-cell tumorigenesis through pro-tumorigenic inflammatory cytokine signalling |journal=Nature Communications |date=27 January 2015 |volume=6 |issue=1 |article-number=6118 |doi=10.1038/ncomms7118  |doi-access=free|pmid=25625625 |pmc=4308813 |bibcode=2015NatCo...6.6118C }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kim |first1=Minchul |last2=Lu |first2=Falong |last3=Zhang |first3=Yi |title=Loss of HDAC-Mediated Repression and Gain of NF-κB Activation Underlie Cytokine Induction in ARID1A- and PIK3CA-Mutation-Driven Ovarian Cancer |journal=Cell Reports |date=September 2016 |volume=17 |issue=1 |pages=275–288 |doi=10.1016/j.celrep.2016.09.003 |pmid=27681437 |pmc=7734570 }}</ref> This cooperation between PI3K and ARID1A is prominent because PIK3CA supplies proliferation of cells and the loss of ARID1A changes the interactions between enhancers and inflammation within the cells, creating a feed-forward loop between IL-6, STAT3 and NFkB.<ref name="auto14"/> Namely, when ARID1A and PIK3CA are mutated, there is a promotion of IL-6 and activation of STAT3 because of the newly established inflammatory [[cytokine]] loop. In this signalling state, the interaction increases between the tumor and the environment surrounding it, recruiting pro-tumor [[myeloid cells]].<ref name="auto14"/>

Because ARID1A loss with PIK3CA activation increases PI3K–AKT signalling, EZH2 blockade that restores the [[endogenous]] inhibitor PIK3IP1 provides a PIK3IP1-dependent vulnerability in ARID1A-mutant systems.<ref name="auto9">{{cite journal |last1=Bitler |first1=Benjamin G |last2=Aird |first2=Katherine M |last3=Garipov |first3=Azat |last4=Li |first4=Hua |last5=Amatangelo |first5=Michael |last6=Kossenkov |first6=Andrew V |last7=Schultz |first7=David C |last8=Liu |first8=Qin |last9=Shih |first9=Ie-Ming |last10=Conejo-Garcia |first10=Jose R |last11=Speicher |first11=David W |last12=Zhang |first12=Rugang |title=Synthetic lethality by targeting EZH2 methyltransferase activity in ARID1A-mutated cancers |journal=Nature Medicine |date=March 2015 |volume=21 |issue=3 |pages=231–238 |doi=10.1038/nm.3799 |pmid=25686104 |pmc=4352133 }}</ref> Through reactivating tumor suppressors like PIK3IP1, which can represent a potential therapeutic strategy due to its ability to limit PI3K–AKT signalling, EZH2 inhibition is able to target specifically cancer cells that are ARID1A-mutant tumors.<ref name="auto9"/>

ARID1A loss also increases the genomic occupancy of CTCF, and this gain contributes to the proliferative advantage of ARID1A-deficient cells; co-occurring CTCF alteration is associated with more favorable clinical outcome than ARID1A loss alone.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Debek |first=Sonia |last2=Meydan |first2=Cem |last3=Chin |first3=Christopher R. |last4=Yu |first4=Jiali |last5=Durmaz |first5=Ceyda |last6=Tsialta |first6=Ioanna |last7=Pelzer |first7=Benedikt |last8=Maurer |first8=Constance |last9=Ou |first9=Arnold |last10=Laguillo-Diego |first10=Alejandra |last11=Scourzic |first11=Laurianne |last12=Tumuluru |first12=Sravya |last13=Teater |first13=Matt |last14=Wu |first14=Rui |last15=Darsow |first15=Brian |date=2026-10-28 |title=SWI/SNF complexes shape 3D chromatin architecture through CTCF and cohesin |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11197-w |journal=Nature |language=en |publisher=Nature Publishing Group |pages=1–12 |doi=10.1038/s41586-026-11197-w |issn=1476-4687}}</ref>

ARID1A deficiency also limits interferon signaling, which is critical in the human immune response, as well as invasion of CD8<sup>+</sup> T-cells.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Watson |first1=Pamela M. |last2=DeVaux |first2=Chelsea A. |last3=Freeman |first3=Kevin W. |title=Loss of ARID1A leads to a cold tumor phenotype via suppression of IFNγ signaling |journal=Scientific Reports |date=13 March 2025 |volume=15 |issue=1 |article-number=8716 |doi=10.1038/s41598-025-91688-4  |doi-access=free|pmid=40082458 |pmc=11906763 |bibcode=2025NatSR..15.8716W }}</ref> Thus, the cell can pass through most immune checkpoints because the loss of ARID1A leads to closing antigen-related enhancers.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Jing |last2=Wang |first2=Weichao |last3=Zhang |first3=Yajia |last4=Cieślik |first4=Marcin |last5=Guo |first5=Jipeng |last6=Tan |first6=Mengyao |last7=Green |first7=Michael D. |last8=Wang |first8=Weimin |last9=Lin |first9=Heng |last10=Li |first10=Wei |last11=Wei |first11=Shuang |last12=Zhou |first12=Jiajia |last13=Li |first13=Gaopeng |last14=Jing |first14=Xiaojun |last15=Vatan |first15=Linda |last16=Zhao |first16=Lili |last17=Bitler |first17=Benjamin |last18=Zhang |first18=Rugang |last19=Cho |first19=Kathleen R. |last20=Dou |first20=Yali |last21=Kryczek |first21=Ilona |last22=Chan |first22=Timothy A. |last23=Huntsman |first23=David |last24=Chinnaiyan |first24=Arul M. |last25=Zou |first25=Weiping |title=Epigenetic driver mutations in ARID1A shape cancer immune phenotype and immunotherapy |journal=Journal of Clinical Investigation |date=20 April 2020 |volume=130 |issue=5 |pages=2712–2726 |doi=10.1172/JCI134402 |pmid=32027624 |pmc=7190935 }}</ref> In prostate tumours, ARID1A deletion combines with loss of PTEN to increase the amount of tumor growth as well as PMN-MDSCs infiltration through continued NF-κB activation.<ref name="auto15">{{cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Ni |last2=Liu |first2=Qiuli |last3=Han |first3=Ying |last4=Pei |first4=Siyu |last5=Cheng |first5=Bisheng |last6=Xu |first6=Junyu |last7=Miao |first7=Xiang |last8=Pan |first8=Qiang |last9=Wang |first9=Hanling |last10=Guo |first10=Jiacheng |last11=Wang |first11=Xuege |last12=Zhang |first12=Guoying |last13=Lian |first13=Yannan |last14=Zhang |first14=Wei |last15=Zang |first15=Yi |last16=Tan |first16=Minjia |last17=Li |first17=Qintong |last18=Wang |first18=Xiaoming |last19=Xiao |first19=Yichuan |last20=Hu |first20=Guohong |last21=Jiang |first21=Jun |last22=Huang |first22=Hai |last23=Qin |first23=Jun |title=ARID1A loss induces polymorphonuclear myeloid-derived suppressor cell chemotaxis and promotes prostate cancer progression |journal=Nature Communications |date=26 November 2022 |volume=13 |issue=1 |article-number=7281 |doi=10.1038/s41467-022-34871-9  |doi-access=free|pmid=36435834 |pmc=9701216 |bibcode=2022NatCo..13.7281L }}</ref> Inflammatory IKKβ signalling [[phosphorylation|phosphorylates]] ARID1A, degrading the protein as well as silencing of A20 enhancer activity, which further boosts suppression from immune NF-κB, creating a feedback loop of NF-κB.<ref name="auto15" />

=== SMARCB1 and rhabdoid tumors ===
In [[malignant rhabdoid tumors]] (MRTs) as well as atypical teratoid or rhabdoid tumors (ATRTs), SMARCB1 acts as a major tumor suppressor that shows bi-allelic inactivation in almost 95% of patients.<ref name="auto16">{{cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=Xiaofeng |last2=Wang |first2=Su |last3=Troisi |first3=Emma C. |last4=Howard |first4=Thomas P. |last5=Haswell |first5=Jeffrey R. |last6=Wolf |first6=Bennett K. |last7=Hawk |first7=William H. |last8=Ramos |first8=Pilar |last9=Oberlick |first9=Elaine M. |last10=Tzvetkov |first10=Evgeni P. |last11=Ross |first11=Aaron |last12=Vazquez |first12=Francisca |last13=Hahn |first13=William C. |last14=Park |first14=Peter J. |last15=Roberts |first15=Charles W. M. |title=BRD9 defines a SWI/SNF sub-complex and constitutes a specific vulnerability in malignant rhabdoid tumors |journal=Nature Communications |date=23 April 2019 |volume=10 |issue=1 |article-number=1881 |doi=10.1038/s41467-019-09891-7  |doi-access=free|pmid=31015438 |pmc=6479050 |bibcode=2019NatCo..10.1881W }}</ref> Without SMARCB1, the chromatin is repressed by Polycomb repressive complexes because SMARCB1 can mark specific genes for silencing and can even compact the chromatin further.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Nakayama |first1=Robert T |last2=Pulice |first2=John L |last3=Valencia |first3=Alfredo M |last4=McBride |first4=Matthew J |last5=McKenzie |first5=Zachary M |last6=Gillespie |first6=Mark A |last7=Ku |first7=Wai Lim |last8=Teng |first8=Mingxiang |last9=Cui |first9=Kairong |last10=Williams |first10=Robert T |last11=Cassel |first11=Seth H |last12=Qing |first12=He |last13=Widmer |first13=Christian J |last14=Demetri |first14=George D |last15=Irizarry |first15=Rafael A |last16=Zhao |first16=Keji |last17=Ranish |first17=Jeffrey A |last18=Kadoch |first18=Cigall |title=SMARCB1 is required for widespread BAF complex–mediated activation of enhancers and bivalent promoters |journal=Nature Genetics |date=November 2017 |volume=49 |issue=11 |pages=1613–1623 |doi=10.1038/ng.3958 |pmid=28945250 |pmc=5803080 }}</ref> In contrast to most cases of cancer, invasive childhood neurological malignancies can arise through even the smallest change, sometimes with SMARCB1 as the sole driver event.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Erkek |first1=Serap |last2=Johann |first2=Pascal D. |last3=Finetti |first3=Martina A. |last4=Drosos |first4=Yiannis |last5=Chou |first5=Hsien-Chao |last6=Zapatka |first6=Marc |last7=Sturm |first7=Dominik |last8=Jones |first8=David T.W. |last9=Korshunov |first9=Andrey |last10=Rhyzova |first10=Marina |last11=Wolf |first11=Stephan |last12=Mallm |first12=Jan-Philipp |last13=Beck |first13=Katja |last14=Witt |first14=Olaf |last15=Kulozik |first15=Andreas E. |last16=Frühwald |first16=Michael C. |last17=Northcott |first17=Paul A. |last18=Korbel |first18=Jan O. |last19=Lichter |first19=Peter |last20=Eils |first20=Roland |last21=Gajjar |first21=Amar |last22=Roberts |first22=Charles W.M. |last23=Williamson |first23=Daniel |last24=Hasselblatt |first24=Martin |last25=Chavez |first25=Lukas |last26=Pfister |first26=Stefan M. |last27=Kool |first27=Marcel |title=Comprehensive Analysis of Chromatin States in Atypical Teratoid/Rhabdoid Tumor Identifies Diverging Roles for SWI/SNF and Polycomb in Gene Regulation |journal=Cancer Cell |date=January 2019 |volume=35 |issue=1 |pages=95–110.e8 |doi=10.1016/j.ccell.2018.11.014 |pmid=30595504 |pmc=6341227 }}</ref> In fact, studies in mice have demonstrated that SMARCB1 functions as a tumor suppressor with two major pathways for destruction: if deleted heterozygously, there could be sporadic MRT development, whereas conditional homozygous loss leads to fast-spreading [[lymphomas]], demonstrating that SMARCB1 by itself can be enough for tumorigenesis.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Klein |first1=Ulf |last2=Tu |first2=Yuhai |last3=Stolovitzky |first3=Gustavo A. |last4=Keller |first4=Jeffrey L. |last5=Haddad |first5=Joseph |last6=Miljkovic |first6=Vladan |last7=Cattoretti |first7=Giorgio |last8=Califano |first8=Andrea |last9=Dalla-Favera |first9=Riccardo |title=Transcriptional analysis of the B cell germinal center reaction |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=4 March 2003 |volume=100 |issue=5 |pages=2639–2644 |doi=10.1073/pnas.0437996100 |pmid=12604779 |pmc=151393 |bibcode=2003PNAS..100.2639K |doi-access=free }}</ref> Rhabdoid tumorigenesis, however, can only happen in particular parts of development.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vitte |first1=Jeremie |last2=Gao |first2=Fuying |last3=Coppola |first3=Giovanni |last4=Judkins |first4=Alexander R. |last5=Giovannini |first5=Marco |title=Timing of Smarcb1 and Nf2 inactivation determines schwannoma versus rhabdoid tumor development |journal=Nature Communications |date=21 August 2017 |volume=8 |issue=1 |article-number=300 |doi=10.1038/s41467-017-00346-5  |doi-access=free|pmid=28824165 |pmc=5563506 |bibcode=2017NatCo...8..300V }}</ref> For instance, the loss of SMARCB1 affects embryonic progenitors, like neural stem cells or primordial germ cells, more than already differentiated cells, indicating the role of age in tissue tropism of RTs.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Graf |first1=Monika |last2=Interlandi |first2=Marta |last3=Moreno |first3=Natalia |last4=Holdhof |first4=Dörthe |last5=Göbel |first5=Carolin |last6=Melcher |first6=Viktoria |last7=Mertins |first7=Julius |last8=Albert |first8=Thomas K. |last9=Kastrati |first9=Dennis |last10=Alfert |first10=Amelie |last11=Holsten |first11=Till |last12=de Faria |first12=Flavia |last13=Meisterernst |first13=Michael |last14=Rossig |first14=Claudia |last15=Warmuth-Metz |first15=Monika |last16=Nowak |first16=Johannes |last17=Meyer zu Hörste |first17=Gerd |last18=Mayère |first18=Chloe |last19=Nef |first19=Serge |last20=Johann |first20=Pascal |last21=Frühwald |first21=Michael C. |last22=Dugas |first22=Martin |last23=Schüller |first23=Ulrich |last24=Kerl |first24=Kornelius |title=Single-cell transcriptomics identifies potential cells of origin of MYC rhabdoid tumors |journal=Nature Communications |date=22 March 2022 |volume=13 |issue=1 |article-number=1544 |doi=10.1038/s41467-022-29152-4  |doi-access=free|pmid=35318328 |pmc=8941154 |bibcode=2022NatCo..13.1544G }}</ref> The activity of enhancers requires SMARCB1, and, as evidence, its loss can stifle the ability of genes to allow cells to differentiate.<ref name="auto16"/> Consequently, rhabdoid tumor cells are specifically vulnerable to the ratios of other SWI/SNF proteins. For instance, rhabdoid tumors without SMARCB1 specifically require BRD9, with BRD9 inhibition leading to cytotoxicity within these cells.<ref name="auto16"/> By contrast, the loss of SMARCB1 lowers the ability of the BAF complex to antagonize Polycomb repressive complexes, expanding the domain of H3K27me3 domains by allowing PRC2 to spread H3K27me3 at promoters where there are differentiation genes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Alfert |first1=Amelie |last2=Walter |first2=Carolin |last3=Moreno |first3=Natalia |last4=Melcher |first4=Viktoria |last5=Graf |first5=Monika |last6=Hotfilder |first6=Marc |last7=Dugas |first7=Martin |last8=Albert |first8=Thomas |last9=Kerl |first9=Kornelius |title=Smarcb1 Loss Results in a Deregulation of esBAF Binding and Impacts the Expression of Neurodevelopmental Genes |journal=Cells |date=15 April 2022 |volume=11 |issue=8 |page=1354 |doi=10.3390/cells11081354 |pmid=35456033 |pmc=9027123 |doi-access=free }}</ref> This creates a therapeutic vulnerability to EZH2 inhibition, a strategy that is supported by clinical evaluation with agents such as tazemetostat.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Knutson |first1=Sarah K. |last2=Warholic |first2=Natalie M. |last3=Wigle |first3=Tim J. |last4=Klaus |first4=Christine R. |last5=Allain |first5=Christina J. |last6=Raimondi |first6=Alejandra |last7=Porter Scott |first7=Margaret |last8=Chesworth |first8=Richard |last9=Moyer |first9=Mikel P. |last10=Copeland |first10=Robert A. |last11=Richon |first11=Victoria M. |last12=Pollock |first12=Roy M. |last13=Kuntz |first13=Kevin W. |last14=Keilhack |first14=Heike |title=Durable tumor regression in genetically altered malignant rhabdoid tumors by inhibition of methyltransferase EZH2 |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=7 May 2013 |volume=110 |issue=19 |pages=7922–7927 |doi=10.1073/pnas.1303800110 |pmid=23620515 |pmc=3651445 |bibcode=2013PNAS..110.7922K |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kurmasheva |first1=Raushan T. |last2=Erickson |first2=Stephen W. |last3=Earley |first3=Eric |last4=Smith |first4=Malcolm A. |last5=Houghton |first5=Peter J. |title=In vivo evaluation of the EZH2 inhibitor (EPZ011989) alone or in combination with standard of care cytotoxic agents against pediatric malignant rhabdoid tumor preclinical models—A report from the Pediatric Preclinical Testing Consortium |journal=Pediatric Blood & Cancer |date=February 2021 |volume=68 |issue=2 |article-number=e28772 |doi=10.1002/pbc.28772 |pmid=33089597 |pmc=10276573 }}</ref> Although rare cases can include the loss of SMARCA4 rather than SMARCB1, the resulting defects in chromatin remodeling are very similar, indicating potentially shared therapeutic targets.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vande Walle |first1=Lieselotte |last2=Van Opdenbosch |first2=Nina |last3=Jacques |first3=Peggy |last4=Fossoul |first4=Amelie |last5=Verheugen |first5=Eveline |last6=Vogel |first6=Peter |last7=Beyaert |first7=Rudi |last8=Elewaut |first8=Dirk |last9=Kanneganti |first9=Thirumala-Devi |last10=van Loo |first10=Geert |last11=Lamkanfi |first11=Mohamed |title=Negative regulation of the NLRP3 inflammasome by A20 protects against arthritis |journal=Nature |date=August 2014 |volume=512 |issue=7512 |pages=69–73 |doi=10.1038/nature13322 |pmid=25043000 |pmc=4126806 |bibcode=2014Natur.512...69W }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Holdhof |first1=Dörthe |last2=Johann |first2=Pascal D. |last3=Spohn |first3=Michael |last4=Bockmayr |first4=Michael |last5=Safaei |first5=Sepehr |last6=Joshi |first6=Piyush |last7=Masliah-Planchon |first7=Julien |last8=Ho |first8=Ben |last9=Andrianteranagna |first9=Mamy |last10=Bourdeaut |first10=Franck |last11=Huang |first11=Annie |last12=Kool |first12=Marcel |last13=Upadhyaya |first13=Santhosh A. |last14=Bendel |first14=Anne E. |last15=Indenbirken |first15=Daniela |last16=Foulkes |first16=William D. |last17=Bush |first17=Jonathan W. |last18=Creytens |first18=David |last19=Kordes |first19=Uwe |last20=Frühwald |first20=Michael C. |last21=Hasselblatt |first21=Martin |last22=Schüller |first22=Ulrich |title=Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumors (ATRTs) with SMARCA4 mutation are molecularly distinct from SMARCB1-deficient cases |journal=Acta Neuropathologica |date=February 2021 |volume=141 |issue=2 |pages=291–301 |doi=10.1007/s00401-020-02250-7 |pmid=33331994 |pmc=7847432 }}</ref>

=== SMARCA4 and cancer ===
SMARCA4, otherwise called BRG1, is one of the most integral tumor suppressors in the BAF complex, and its loss can lead to mechanistically different but functionally similar types of aggressive malignancies.<ref name="auto19">{{cite journal |last1=Jelinic |first1=Petar |last2=Mueller |first2=Jennifer J |last3=Olvera |first3=Narciso |last4=Dao |first4=Fanny |last5=Scott |first5=Sasinya N |last6=Shah |first6=Ronak |last7=Gao |first7=JianJiong |last8=Schultz |first8=Nikolaus |last9=Gonen |first9=Mithat |last10=Soslow |first10=Robert A |last11=Berger |first11=Michael F |last12=Levine |first12=Douglas A |title=Recurrent SMARCA4 mutations in small cell carcinoma of the ovary |journal=Nature Genetics |date=May 2014 |volume=46 |issue=5 |pages=424–426 |doi=10.1038/ng.2922 |pmid=24658004 |pmc=5699446 }}</ref> In hypercalcemic ovarian small cell carcinoma, biallelic mutations of SMARCA4 gene, which leads to the loss of BRG1, are extremely widespread, as shown in ovary small cell carcinoma, where 94% of the cases have SMARCA4 mutation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Duan |first1=Huanli |last2=Gao |first2=Wei |last3=Wang |first3=Leiming |last4=Cao |first4=Feng |last5=Teng |first5=Lianghong |title=Undifferentiated colonic neoplasm with SMARCA4 germline gene mutation and loss of SMARCA4 protein expression: a case report and literature review |journal=Diagnostic Pathology |date=December 2021 |volume=16 |issue=1 |article-number=30 |doi=10.1186/s13000-021-01091-6 |pmid=33836796 |pmc=8033741 |doi-access=free }}</ref> In fact, identification of the genetic status of SMARCA4 is one of the criteria/the main criterion for confirming the disease.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tischkowitz |first1=Marc |last2=Huang |first2=Sidong |last3=Banerjee |first3=Susana |last4=Hague |first4=Jennifer |last5=Hendricks |first5=William P.D. |last6=Huntsman |first6=David G. |last7=Lang |first7=Jessica D. |last8=Orlando |first8=Krystal A. |last9=Oza |first9=Amit M. |last10=Pautier |first10=Patricia |last11=Ray-Coquard |first11=Isabelle |last12=Trent |first12=Jeffrey M. |last13=Witcher |first13=Michael |last14=Witkowski |first14=Leora |last15=McCluggage |first15=W. Glenn |last16=Levine |first16=Douglas A. |last17=Foulkes |first17=William D. |last18=Weissman |first18=Bernard E. |title=Small-Cell Carcinoma of the Ovary, Hypercalcemic Type–Genetics, New Treatment Targets, and Current Management Guidelines |journal=Clinical Cancer Research |date=1 August 2020 |volume=26 |issue=15 |pages=3908–3917 |doi=10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-19-3797 |pmid=32156746 |pmc=7415570 }}</ref> Moreover, blocking a second, compensatory pathway that the cells rely on after the first copy of the gene is lost stands out as a therapeutic possibility.<ref name="auto19"/> When SMARCA4 is lost, the ATPase activity of the BAF complex is compromised, with only SMARCA2 compensating as the ATPase subunit in SMARCA4-dependent cells, resulting in altered enhancers and promoter accessibility, so transcription factors that would otherwise have access to DNA no longer reach their targets.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wilson |first1=Boris G. |last2=Helming |first2=Katherine C. |last3=Wang |first3=Xiaofeng |last4=Kim |first4=Youngha |last5=Vazquez |first5=Francisca |last6=Jagani |first6=Zainab |last7=Hahn |first7=William C. |last8=Roberts |first8=Charles W. M. |title=Residual Complexes Containing SMARCA2 (BRM) Underlie the Oncogenic Drive of SMARCA4 ( BRG1 ) Mutation |journal=Molecular and Cellular Biology |date=1 March 2014 |volume=34 |issue=6 |pages=1136–1144 |doi=10.1128/MCB.01372-13 |pmid=24421395 |pmc=3958034 }}</ref> In non-small cell lung cancer, SMARCA4 mutations are present in about 5–10% of tumors, especially in those with undifferentiated or rhabdoid phenotype.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Longo |first1=Vito |last2=Catino |first2=Annamaria |last3=Montrone |first3=Michele |last4=Montagna |first4=Elisabetta Sara |last5=Pesola |first5=Francesco |last6=Marech |first6=Ilaria |last7=Pizzutilo |first7=Pamela |last8=Nardone |first8=Annalisa |last9=Perrone |first9=Antonella |last10=Gesualdo |first10=Monica |last11=Galetta |first11=Domenico |title=Treatment of Thoracic SMARCA4-Deficient Undifferentiated Tumors: Where We Are and Where We Will Go |journal=International Journal of Molecular Sciences |date=13 March 2024 |volume=25 |issue=6 |page=3237 |doi=10.3390/ijms25063237 |pmid=38542211 |pmc=10970296 |doi-access=free }}</ref> At a more cellular level, BRG1 loss disrupts chromatin accessibility that cells obtain from SWI/SNF, reducing DNA repair through p53 and BRCA1, as well as activating previously inactivated oncogenic MYC programs.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bhat-Nakshatri |first1=Poornima |last2=Khatpe |first2=Aditi S. |last3=Chen |first3=Duojiao |last4=Batic |first4=Katie |last5=Mang |first5=Henry |last6=Herodotou |first6=Christopher |last7=McGuire |first7=Patrick C. |last8=Xuei |first8=Xiaoling |last9=Erdogan |first9=Cihat |last10=Gao |first10=Hongyu |last11=Liu |first11=Yunlong |last12=Sandusky |first12=George |last13=Storniolo |first13=Anna Maria |last14=Nakshatri |first14=Harikrishna |title=Signaling Pathway Alterations Driven by BRCA1 and BRCA2 Germline Mutations are Sufficient to Initiate Breast Tumorigenesis by the PIK3CAH1047R Oncogene |journal=Cancer Research Communications |date=5 January 2024 |volume=4 |issue=1 |pages=38–54 |doi=10.1158/2767-9764.CRC-23-0330 |pmid=38059556 |pmc=10774565 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bochar |first1=Daniel A |last2=Wang |first2=Lai |last3=Beniya |first3=Hideo |last4=Kinev |first4=Alexander |last5=Xue |first5=Yutong |last6=Lane |first6=William S |last7=Wang |first7=Weidong |last8=Kashanchi |first8=Fatah |last9=Shiekhattar |first9=Ramin |title=BRCA1 Is Associated with a Human SWI/SNF-Related Complex |journal=Cell |date=July 2000 |volume=102 |issue=2 |pages=257–265 |doi=10.1016/S0092-8674(00)00030-1 |pmid=10943845 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Orvis |first1=Tess |last2=Hepperla |first2=Austin |last3=Walter |first3=Vonn |last4=Song |first4=Shujie |last5=Simon |first5=Jeremy |last6=Parker |first6=Joel |last7=Wilkerson |first7=Matthew D. |last8=Desai |first8=Nisarg |last9=Major |first9=Michael B. |last10=Hayes |first10=D. Neil |last11=Davis |first11=Ian J. |last12=Weissman |first12=Bernard |title=BRG1/SMARCA4 Inactivation Promotes Non–Small Cell Lung Cancer Aggressiveness by Altering Chromatin Organization |journal=Cancer Research |date=15 November 2014 |volume=74 |issue=22 |pages=6486–6498 |doi=10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-14-0061 |pmid=25115300 |pmc=4233181 }}</ref> Activation of MYC programs accelerates the cell cycle, increasing transcriptional demand, which promotes growth but also elevates [[replication stress]]. These changes collectively destabilize the genome and lead to dedifferentiation through loss of H3K27 acetylation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Concepcion |first1=Carla P. |last2=Ma |first2=Sai |last3=LaFave |first3=Lindsay M. |last4=Bhutkar |first4=Arjun |last5=Liu |first5=Manyuan |last6=DeAngelo |first6=Lydia P. |last7=Kim |first7=Jonathan Y. |last8=Del Priore |first8=Isabella |last9=Schoenfeld |first9=Adam J. |last10=Miller |first10=Manon |last11=Kartha |first11=Vinay K. |last12=Westcott |first12=Peter M.K. |last13=Sánchez-Rivera |first13=Francisco J. |last14=Meli |first14=Kevin |last15=Gupta |first15=Manav |last16=Bronson |first16=Roderick T. |last17=Riely |first17=Gregory J. |last18=Rekhtman |first18=Natasha |last19=Rudin |first19=Charles M. |last20=Kim |first20=Carla F. |last21=Regev |first21=Aviv |last22=Buenrostro |first22=Jason D. |last23=Jacks |first23=Tyler |title=Smarca4 Inactivation Promotes Lineage-Specific Transformation and Early Metastatic Features in the Lung |journal=Cancer Discovery |date=1 February 2022 |volume=12 |issue=2 |pages=562–585 |doi=10.1158/2159-8290.CD-21-0248 |pmid=34561242 |pmc=8831463 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Shi |first1=X |last2=Wang |first2=Q |last3=Gu |first3=J |last4=Xuan |first4=Z |last5=Wu |first5=J I |title=SMARCA4/Brg1 coordinates genetic and epigenetic networks underlying Shh-type medulloblastoma development |journal=Oncogene |date=3 November 2016 |volume=35 |issue=44 |pages=5746–5758 |doi=10.1038/onc.2016.108 |pmid=27065321 }}</ref> These mutations may lead to therapeutic susceptibilities, where SMARCA4-deficient cells face replication stress as well as chromatin compaction, making them highly sensitive to ATR inhibitors.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kurashima |first1=Kiminori |last2=Kashiwagi |first2=Hideto |last3=Shimomura |first3=Iwao |last4=Suzuki |first4=Ayako |last5=Takeshita |first5=Fumitaka |last6=Mazevet |first6=Marianne |last7=Harata |first7=Masahiko |last8=Yamashita |first8=Takayuki |last9=Yamamoto |first9=Yusuke |last10=Kohno |first10=Takashi |last11=Shiotani |first11=Bunsyo |title=SMARCA4 deficiency-associated heterochromatin induces intrinsic DNA replication stress and susceptibility to ATR inhibition in lung adenocarcinoma |journal=NAR Cancer |date=1 June 2020 |volume=2 |issue=2 |article-number=zcaa005 |doi=10.1093/narcan/zcaa005 |pmid=34316685 |pmc=8210217 }}</ref> The said replication stress can cause DNA breaks and chromosome instability that drive tumor initiation.

=== Therapeutic strategies ===
Mutations of the SWI/SNF complex have provided the initial push for finding therapeutic measures to target cancers where ARID1A is mutated.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Zhang |first1=Xuewei |last2=Zhang |first2=Yixuan |last3=Zhao |first3=Jinyi |last4=Wu |first4=Yinjie |last5=Zhang |first5=Na |last6=Shen |first6=Wenjing |title=ARID1A mutations in cancer development: mechanism and therapy |journal=Carcinogenesis |date=27 May 2023 |volume=44 |issue=3 |pages=197–208 |doi=10.1093/carcin/bgad011 |pmid=36882165 }}</ref> Common ARID1A loss-of-function cancers allow for specific properties that are being examined to understand more about the role of mutations in subunits of SWI/SNF in the proliferation of cancer cells.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Xu |first1=Shouying |last2=Tang |first2=Chao |title=The Role of ARID1A in Tumors: Tumor Initiation or Tumor Suppression? |journal=Frontiers in Oncology |date=4 October 2021 |volume=11 |article-number=745187 |doi=10.3389/fonc.2021.745187 |pmid=34671561 |pmc=8521028 |doi-access=free }}</ref> [[Epigenetics|Epigenetic]] therapy is one of the advancements made in this matter, where it was found that cancer cells lacking ARID1A can die when EZH2 is inhibited.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Alldredge |first1=Jill K. |last2=Eskander |first2=Ramez N. |title=EZH2 inhibition in ARID1A mutated clear cell and endometrioid ovarian and endometrioid endometrial cancers |journal=Gynecologic Oncology Research and Practice |date=December 2017 |volume=4 |issue=1 |article-number=17 |doi=10.1186/s40661-017-0052-y |pmid=29093822 |pmc=5663065 |doi-access=free }}</ref> In particular, ARID1A-mutant ovarian clear cell carcinomas show dramatic regression when there is the inhibition of EZH2.<ref name="auto9"/> ARID1A-loss malignancies are similarly very reliant on ATR-mediated DNA damage checkpoints.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Shen |first1=Jianfeng |last2=Peng |first2=Yang |last3=Wei |first3=Leizhen |last4=Zhang |first4=Wei |last5=Yang |first5=Lin |last6=Lan |first6=Li |last7=Kapoor |first7=Prabodh |last8=Ju |first8=Zhenlin |last9=Mo |first9=Qianxing |last10=Shih |first10=Ie-Ming |last11=Uray |first11=Ivan P. |last12=Wu |first12=Xiangwei |last13=Brown |first13=Powel H. |last14=Shen |first14=Xuetong |last15=Mills |first15=Gordon B. |last16=Peng |first16=Guang |title=ARID1A Deficiency Impairs the DNA Damage Checkpoint and Sensitizes Cells to PARP Inhibitors |journal=Cancer Discovery |date=1 July 2015 |volume=5 |issue=7 |pages=752–767 |doi=10.1158/2159-8290.CD-14-0849 |pmid=26069190 |pmc=4497871 }}</ref> As such, inhibition of the ATR kinase acts specifically to induce DNA damage, premature [[mitosis]], and [[apoptosis|cell death]] of ARID1A-loss models, which has prompted clinical studies.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Williamson |first1=Chris T. |last2=Miller |first2=Rowan |last3=Pemberton |first3=Helen N. |last4=Jones |first4=Samuel E. |last5=Campbell |first5=James |last6=Konde |first6=Asha |last7=Badham |first7=Nicholas |last8=Rafiq |first8=Rumana |last9=Brough |first9=Rachel |last10=Gulati |first10=Aditi |last11=Ryan |first11=Colm J. |last12=Francis |first12=Jeff |last13=Vermulen |first13=Peter B. |last14=Reynolds |first14=Andrew R. |last15=Reaper |first15=Philip M. |last16=Pollard |first16=John R. |last17=Ashworth |first17=Alan |last18=Lord |first18=Christopher J. |title=ATR inhibitors as a synthetic lethal therapy for tumours deficient in ARID1A |journal=Nature Communications |date=13 December 2016 |volume=7 |issue=1 |article-number=13837 |doi=10.1038/ncomms13837  |doi-access=free|pmid=27958275 |pmc=5159945 |bibcode=2016NatCo...713837W }}</ref> ARID1A loss decreases homologous recombination repair, which repairs harmful DNA breaks, so the cells are acutely susceptible to inhibition of PARP, a protein found in the cell nucleus that can detect said DNA damage.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wu |first1=Changjie |last2=Lyu |first2=Junfang |last3=Yang |first3=Eun Ju |last4=Liu |first4=Yifan |last5=Zhang |first5=Baoyuan |last6=Shim |first6=Joong Sup |title=Targeting AURKA-CDC25C axis to induce synthetic lethality in ARID1A-deficient colorectal cancer cells |journal=Nature Communications |date=10 August 2018 |volume=9 |issue=1 |article-number=3212 |doi=10.1038/s41467-018-05694-4  |doi-access=free|pmid=30097580 |pmc=6086874 |bibcode=2018NatCo...9.3212W }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Yakovlev |first1=Vasily A. |last2=Sullivan |first2=Stephanie A. |last3=Fields |first3=Emma C. |last4=Temkin |first4=Sarah M. |title=PARP inhibitors in the treatment of ARID1A mutant ovarian clear cell cancer: PI3K/Akt1-dependent mechanism of synthetic lethality |journal=Frontiers in Oncology |date=22 February 2023 |volume=13 |article-number=1124147 |doi=10.3389/fonc.2023.1124147 |pmid=36910637 |pmc=9992988 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

Besides impacting the cell cycle alongside DNA repair tracks, ARID1A inactivation produces metabolic instabilities as well.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kearns |first1=Bridger |last2=McKell |first2=Andralyn |last3=Steveson |first3=Isaac |last4=Worley |first4=Peyton |last5=Barton |first5=Braeden |last6=Bennett |first6=Jordan |last7=Anderson |first7=DeLaney |last8=Harris |first8=Jacob |last9=Christensen |first9=James |last10=Barrott |first10=Jared J. |title=ARID1A and Its Impact Across the Hallmarks of Cancer |journal=International Journal of Molecular Sciences |date=13 May 2025 |volume=26 |issue=10 |page=4644 |doi=10.3390/ijms26104644 |pmid=40429787 |pmc=12111594 |doi-access=free }}</ref> ARID1A-mutant tumors can become addicted to glutamine as a side-product of glycolysis down-regulation in their cells as well as enhanced glutaminase dependence. Therefore, inhibition of GLS1, the enzyme that converts glutamine to glutamate, causes metabolic collapse.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wu |first1=Shuai |last2=Fukumoto |first2=Takeshi |last3=Lin |first3=Jianhuang |last4=Nacarelli |first4=Timothy |last5=Wang |first5=Yemin |last6=Ong |first6=Dionzie |last7=Liu |first7=Heng |last8=Fatkhutdinov |first8=Nail |last9=Zundell |first9=Joseph A. |last10=Karakashev |first10=Sergey |last11=Zhou |first11=Wei |last12=Schwartz |first12=Lauren E. |last13=Tang |first13=Hsin-Yao |last14=Drapkin |first14=Ronny |last15=Liu |first15=Qin |last16=Huntsman |first16=David G. |last17=Kossenkov |first17=Andrew V. |last18=Speicher |first18=David W. |last19=Schug |first19=Zachary T. |last20=Van Dang |first20=Chi |last21=Zhang |first21=Rugang |title=Targeting glutamine dependence through GLS1 inhibition suppresses ARID1A-inactivated clear cell ovarian carcinoma |journal=Nature Cancer |date=11 January 2021 |volume=2 |issue=2 |pages=189–200 |doi=10.1038/s43018-020-00160-x |pmid=34085048 |pmc=8168620 }}</ref> Other BAF complex subunits are also being used for research into cancer therapy. In fact, loss of SMARCB1, as seen in cancerous rhabdoid tumors and epithelioid [[sarcoma]], causes early dependence on EZH2 activity.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kazansky |first1=Yaniv |last2=Cameron |first2=Daniel |last3=Mueller |first3=Helen S. |last4=Demarest |first4=Phillip |last5=Zaffaroni |first5=Nadia |last6=Arrighetti |first6=Noemi |last7=Zuco |first7=Valentina |last8=Kuwahara |first8=Yasumichi |last9=Somwar |first9=Romel |last10=Ladanyi |first10=Marc |last11=Qu |first11=Rui |last12=de Stanchina |first12=Elisa |last13=Dela Cruz |first13=Filemon S. |last14=Kung |first14=Andrew L. |last15=Gounder |first15=Mrinal M. |last16=Kentsis |first16=Alex |title=Overcoming Clinical Resistance to EZH2 Inhibition Using Rational Epigenetic Combination Therapy |journal=Cancer Discovery |date=3 June 2024 |volume=14 |issue=6 |pages=965–981 |doi=10.1158/2159-8290.CD-23-0110 |pmid=38315003 |pmc=11147720 }}</ref> Similarly, inhibition of EZH2 by the EZH2 inhibitor tazemetostat has shown clinical activity as an approved treatment of SMARCB1-deficient sarcomas.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gounder |first1=Mrinal |last2=Schöffski |first2=Patrick |last3=Jones |first3=Robin L |last4=Agulnik |first4=Mark |last5=Cote |first5=Gregory M |last6=Villalobos |first6=Victor M |last7=Attia |first7=Steven |last8=Chugh |first8=Rashmi |last9=Chen |first9=Tom Wei-Wu |last10=Jahan |first10=Thierry |last11=Loggers |first11=Elizabeth T |last12=Gupta |first12=Abha |last13=Italiano |first13=Antoine |last14=Demetri |first14=George D |last15=Ratan |first15=Ravin |last16=Davis |first16=Lara E |last17=Mir |first17=Olivier |last18=Dileo |first18=Palma |last19=Van Tine |first19=Brian A |last20=Pressey |first20=Joseph G |last21=Lingaraj |first21=Trupti |last22=Rajarethinam |first22=Anand |last23=Sierra |first23=Laura |last24=Agarwal |first24=Shefali |last25=Stacchiotti |first25=Silvia |title=Tazemetostat in advanced epithelioid sarcoma with loss of INI1/SMARCB1: an international, open-label, phase 2 basket study |journal=The Lancet Oncology |date=November 2020 |volume=21 |issue=11 |pages=1423–1432 |doi=10.1016/S1470-2045(20)30451-4 |pmid=33035459 }}</ref> Random mutations of the PBAF sub-complex also offer hints at immune therapy since mutations of the PBRM1 gene in renal cell carcinomas are related to improved response to PD-1 blockade, which uses inhibitors that target PD-1 protein in T cells, allowing the immune system to attack the said cancer cells.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Miao |first1=Diana |last2=Margolis |first2=Claire A. |last3=Gao |first3=Wenhua |last4=Voss |first4=Martin H. |last5=Li |first5=Wei |last6=Martini |first6=Dylan J. |last7=Norton |first7=Craig |last8=Bossé |first8=Dominick |last9=Wankowicz |first9=Stephanie M. |last10=Cullen |first10=Dana |last11=Horak |first11=Christine |last12=Wind-Rotolo |first12=Megan |last13=Tracy |first13=Adam |last14=Giannakis |first14=Marios |last15=Hodi |first15=Frank Stephen |last16=Drake |first16=Charles G. |last17=Ball |first17=Mark W. |last18=Allaf |first18=Mohamad E. |last19=Snyder |first19=Alexandra |last20=Hellmann |first20=Matthew D. |last21=Ho |first21=Thai |last22=Motzer |first22=Robert J. |last23=Signoretti |first23=Sabina |last24=Kaelin |first24=William G. |last25=Choueiri |first25=Toni K. |last26=Van Allen |first26=Eliezer M. |title=Genomic correlates of response to immune checkpoint therapies in clear cell renal cell carcinoma |journal=Science |date=16 February 2018 |volume=359 |issue=6377 |pages=801–806 |doi=10.1126/science.aan5951 |pmid=29301960 |pmc=6035749 |bibcode=2018Sci...359..801M }}</ref>

== History ==

SWI/SNF complexes were discovered in the 1980s in yeast, ''Saccharomyces cerevisiae''.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Stern |first1=Michael |last2=Jensen |first2=Robert |last3=Herskowitz |first3=Ira |title=Five SWI genes are required for expression of the HO gene in yeast |journal=Journal of Molecular Biology |date=October 1984 |volume=178 |issue=4 |pages=853–868 |doi=10.1016/0022-2836(84)90315-2 |pmid=6436497 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Neigeborn |first1=Lenore |last2=Carlson |first2=Marian |title=GENES AFFECTING THE REGULATION OF ''SUC2'' GENE EXPRESSION BY GLUCOSE REPRESSION IN ''SACCHAROMYCES CEREVISIAE'' |journal=Genetics |date=1 December 1984 |volume=108 |issue=4 |pages=845–858 |doi=10.1093/genetics/108.4.845 |pmid=6392017 |pmc=1224269 }}</ref> The "SWI" (Switch) and "SNF" (Sucrose Non-Fermenting) genes were named for their roles in mating type switching and sucrose metabolism, respectively. These genes' crucial involvement in chromatin remodeling was revealed when their loss of function interfered with related transcriptional processes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Martens |first1=Joseph A. |last2=Winston |first2=Fred |title=Evidence that Swi/Snf directly represses transcription in S. cerevisiae |journal=Genes & Development |date=1 September 2002 |volume=16 |issue=17 |pages=2231–2236 |doi=10.1101/gad.1009902|pmid=12208846 |pmc=186664 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hirschhorn |first1=J N |last2=Brown |first2=S A |last3=Clark |first3=C D |last4=Winston |first4=F |title=Evidence that SNF2/SWI2 and SNF5 activate transcription in yeast by altering chromatin structure. |journal=Genes & Development |date=1 December 1992 |volume=6 |issue=12a |pages=2288–2298 |doi=10.1101/gad.6.12a.2288 |pmid=1459453 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Cairns |first1=B R |last2=Kim |first2=Y J |last3=Sayre |first3=M H |last4=Laurent |first4=B C |last5=Kornberg |first5=R D |title=A multisubunit complex containing the SWI1/ADR6,SWI2/SNF2, SWI3, SNF5, and SNF6 gene products isolated fromyeast. |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=March 1994 |volume=91 |issue=5 |pages=1950–1954 |doi=10.1073/pnas.91.5.1950 |pmid=8127913 |pmc=43282 |bibcode=1994PNAS...91.1950C |doi-access=free }}</ref> In the late 1980s, complexes analogous to the yeast SWI/SNF were also identified in the fruit fly ''[[Drosophila melanogaster]]'', based on their genetic antagonism to Polycomb-mediated gene repression.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tamkun |first1=John W. |last2=Deuring |first2=Renate |last3=Scott |first3=Matthew P. |last4=Kissinger |first4=Mark |last5=Pattatucci |first5=Angela M. |last6=Kaufman |first6=Thomas C. |last7=Kennison |first7=James A. |title=brahma: A regulator of Drosophila homeotic genes structurally related to the yeast transcriptional activator SNF2SWI2 |journal=Cell |date=February 1992 |volume=68 |issue=3 |pages=561–572 |doi=10.1016/0092-8674(92)90191-E |pmid=1346755 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kennison |first1=J A |last2=Tamkun |first2=J W |title=Dosage-dependent modifiers of polycomb and antennapedia mutations in Drosophila. |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=November 1988 |volume=85 |issue=21 |pages=8136–8140 |doi=10.1073/pnas.85.21.8136 |pmid=3141923 |pmc=282375 |bibcode=1988PNAS...85.8136K |doi-access=free }}</ref> The central ATPase subunit of these complexes is encoded by the brahma (brm) gene, from which the name BAP (Brahma-associated proteins) complex is derived. In addition to BAP, D. melanogaster has a second BRM-containing complex known as PBAP (Polybromo-associated BAP). Although both complexes function to counteract Polycomb silencing, they exhibit distinct roles in different biological processes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Terriente-Félix |first1=Ana |last2=de Celis |first2=Jose F. |title=Osa, a subunit of the BAP chromatin-remodelling complex, participates in the regulation of gene expression in response to EGFR signalling in the Drosophila wing |journal=Developmental Biology |date=May 2009 |volume=329 |issue=2 |pages=350–361 |doi=10.1016/j.ydbio.2009.03.010 |pmid=19306864 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rendina |first1=Rosaria |last2=Strangi |first2=Agostino |last3=Avallone |first3=Bice |last4=Giordano |first4=Ennio |title=Bap170, a Subunit of the Drosophila PBAP Chromatin Remodeling Complex, Negatively Regulates the EGFR Signaling |journal=Genetics |date=1 September 2010 |volume=186 |issue=1 |pages=167–181 |doi=10.1534/genetics.110.118695  |doi-access=free|pmid=20551433 |pmc=2940285 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Molnar |first1=Cristina |last2=López-Varea |first2=Ana |last3=Hernández |first3=Rosario |last4=de Celis |first4=Jose F |title=A Gain-of-Function Screen Identifying Genes Required for Vein Formation in the Drosophila melanogaster Wing |journal=Genetics |date=1 November 2006 |volume=174 |issue=3 |pages=1635–1659 |doi=10.1534/genetics.106.061283 |pmid=16980395 |pmc=1667087 }}</ref>

The identification of SWI/SNF-like complexes in mammalian cells became possible shortly after the molecular cloning of genes homologous to the yeast SWI2/SNF2 genes. Two ATPase subunits of the SNF2 family, BRG1 (Brahma-related gene 1) and hBRM (human Brahma), which use ATP to remodel nucleosomes, were identified in the early 1990s.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Muchardt |first1=C. |last2=Yaniv |first2=M. |last3=Mattei |first3=M.-G. |title=Assignment of HBRM, the human homolog of S. cerevisiae SNF2/SWI2 and Drosophila brm genes, to chromosome region 9p23?p24, by in situ hybridization |journal=Mammalian Genome |date=April 1994 |volume=5 |issue=4 |pages=241–243 |doi=10.1007/BF00360554 |pmid=8012116 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Muchardt |first1=C. |last2=Yaniv |first2=M. |title=A human homologue of Saccharomyces cerevisiae SNF2/SWI2 and Drosophila brm genes potentiates transcriptional activation by the glucocorticoid receptor. |journal=The EMBO Journal |date=November 1993 |volume=12 |issue=11 |pages=4279–4290 |doi=10.1002/j.1460-2075.1993.tb06112.x |pmid=8223438 |pmc=413724 }}</ref> These findings demonstrated that SWI/SNF complexes are evolutionarily conserved in mammals. Subsequent biochemical purification studies revealed the existence of two distinct complexes in mammalian cells containing either BRG1 or BRM, each assembling with different and partially overlapping subunits. These complexes are collectively referred to as BAF. Compared to the yeast SWI/SNF complex, BAF complexes exhibit much greater structural and functional diversity (Table 2). This diversity allows them to regulate specific transcriptional processes across various cell types and developmental stages. To improve upon the comparison between different species that is given from this diversity, the SWI/SNF Infobase was developed as a comprehensive database containing subunit-specific protein and [[nucleic acid]] sequence information from approximately 20 different organisms.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mani |first1=Udayakumar |last2=S. |first2=Alagu Sankareswaran |last3=Goutham R. N. |first3=Arun |last4=Mohan S. |first4=Suma |title=SWI/SNF Infobase—An exclusive information portal for SWI/SNF remodeling complex subunits |journal=PLOS ONE |date=29 September 2017 |volume=12 |issue=9 |article-number=e0184445 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0184445 |pmid=28961249 |pmc=5621669 |bibcode=2017PLoSO..1284445M |doi-access=free }}</ref> Over time, it became evident that these complexes can change significantly, especially in development and differentiation. For example, during [[neural development]], a transition occurs from the neural progenitor BAF complex to the neuronal BAF complex, involving subunit-level changes such as the replacement of BAF53A (ACTL6A) with BAF53B (ACTL6B).<ref name="auto6"/><ref name="auto2"/> In recent years, high-throughput sequencing and genome analyses have further revealed that BAF complexes have tumor suppressor roles. Notably, genes encoding several subunits, including ARID1A, SMARCB1, PBRM1, and SMARCA4 (which encodes BRG1), have been found to be frequently mutated in various human cancers.<ref name="auto5"/><ref name="auto11"/><ref name="auto3"/><ref name="auto10"/>

